45585 true] [17592186294834 1069 "q-276462" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294834 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{{a}^{2}-2a-3}=\\frac{2}{a+1}.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294834 1072 #uuid "9e2d222a-c3c2-5ffa-bef1-7b19d0d1e71f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294834 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)}=\\frac{2}{a+1}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a+1,\\,a-3,\\,(a+1)(a-3))=(a+1)(a-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $a=-1$ and $a=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)} & =\\frac{2}{a+1} \\\\ (a+1)(a-3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)}) & =(a+1)(a-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{a+1} \\\\ \\frac{(a+1)(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{10(a+1)(a-3)}{(a+1)(a-3)} & =\\frac{2(a+1)(a-3)}{a+1} \\\\ \\frac{(a+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(a-3)}}{\\cancel{a-3}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{(a+1)(a-3)}}{\\cancel{(a+1)(a-3)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(a+1)}\\cdot (a-3)}{\\cancel{a+1}} \\\\ a+1-10 & =2(a-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $a,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+1-10 & =2(a-3) \\\\ a-9 & =2a-6 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-3\\ne -1$ and $-3\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $a=-3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $a=-3.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294757 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1069 "q-276463" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+3m-10}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1072 #uuid "2a235bfb-2d10-509e-9062-d04e3bbe81b4" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294835 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}=\\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+5,\\,m-2,\\,(m+5)(m-2))=(m+5)(m-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-5$ and $m=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2} & =\\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ (m+5)(m-2)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}) & =(m+5)(m-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+5)(m-2)}{m+5}+\\frac{(m+5)(m-2)}{m-2} & =\\frac{3(m+5)(m-2)}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+5)}(m-2)}{\\cancel{m+5}}+\\frac{(m+5)\\cancel{(m-2)}}{\\cancel{m-2}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+5)(m-2)}}{\\cancel{(m+5)(m-2)}} \\\\ 3(m-2)+(m+5) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-2)+(m+5) & =3 \\\\ 4m-1 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =4 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\ne -5$ and $1\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $m=1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294025 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1069 "q-276464" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1072 #uuid "29cb21f5-537c-5bc7-a705-96283cfbd594" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294836 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294512 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1069 "q-276466" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}=\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}-3h}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1072 #uuid "1b500e21-4864-5311-8c7d-0f9dd561877c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294837 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}=\\frac{1}{h(2h-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h-3,\\,h(2h-3))=h(2h-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=\\frac{3}{2}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3} & =\\frac{1}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ h(2h-3)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}) & =h(2h-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ \\frac{7h(2h-3)}{h}-\\frac{3h(2h-3)}{2h-3} & =\\frac{h(2h-3)}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ \\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{h}(2h-3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{3h\\cdot \\cancel{(2h-3)}}{\\cancel{2h-3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{h(2h-3)}}{\\cancel{h(2h-3)}} \\\\ 7(2h-3)-3h & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2h-3)-3h & =1 \\\\ 14h-21-3h & =1 \\\\ 11h & =22 \\\\ h & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne 0$ and $2\\ne \\frac{3}{2},$ we conclude that $h=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $h=2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294264 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1069 "q-276476" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the first term is $8$ and the common ratio is $3?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1072 #uuid "f6867e4d-6b39-51f1-8d27-677b5a8ff2b6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294838 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=8$ and $r=3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{8(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{2}(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =4({3}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294604 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1069 "q-276481" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series $5+10+20+⋯?$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1072 #uuid "09e80b68-e37c-585a-8f94-efba65167d77" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294839 1074 "First, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=5$ and $r=2.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{5(1-{2}^{N})}{1-2} \\\\  & =-5(1-{2}^{N}) \\\\  & =5({2}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294636 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1069 "q-276482" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series $6+18+54+⋯?$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1072 #uuid "dd476653-a7fa-592f-889f-112530181968" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294840 1074 "First, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=6$ and $r=3.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{6(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{2}(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =3({3}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294566 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1069 "q-276485" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $4$th term is $324$ and the common ratio is $3?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1072 #uuid "bf7c4c70-e690-5caf-bade-96a1c2ab0781" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294841 1074 "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 324 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{(4-1)} \\\\ 324 & =27{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=12$ and $r=3.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{12(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-{3}^{N})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-6(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =6({3}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294454 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1069 "q-276486" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $4$th term is $320$ and the common ratio is $4?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1072 #uuid "756d1655-3e92-5f87-a4c5-83a95aa7d41f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294842 1074 "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 320 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{(4-1)} \\\\ 320 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=5$ and $r=4.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{5(1-{4}^{N})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(1-{4}^{N})}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}(1-{4}^{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294259 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294317 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1069 "q-276511" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1071 "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1072 #uuid "eb68afad-979d-5067-bbdf-a0270d1ebbf5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294843 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294510 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294530 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1069 "q-276514" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1071 "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1072 #uuid "41b67774-fd1a-58ed-b47c-db421e0a8ae7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294844 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{3},-2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293966 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1069 "q-276549" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}-4}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1072 #uuid "1df5a9d5-3792-5d51-9c6f-6fceb73b5df5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294845 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+2,\\,y-2,\\,(y-2)(y+2))=(y-2)(y+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+2} & =\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)(y+2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{y+2} & =(y-2)(y+2)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}) \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)(y+2)}{y+2} & =\\frac{7(y-2)(y+2)}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{(y-2)(y+2)}{y-2} \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)\\cancel{(y+2)}}{\\cancel{y+2}} & =\\frac{7\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}{\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}-\\frac{\\cancel{(y-2)}(y+2)}{\\cancel{y-2}} \\\\ 3(y-2) & =7-(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(y-2) & =7-(y+2) \\\\ 3y-6 & =7-y-2 \\\\ 4y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne -2$ and $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $y=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $y=\\frac{11}{4}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294730 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1069 "q-276552" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-25}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.$\n\n$w=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1072 #uuid "f8c5f842-2a93-591d-a877-d0b5aa60a9f2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294846 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,w+5,\\,(w-5)(w+5))=(w-5)(w+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=-5$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5} \\\\ (w-5)(w+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =(w-5)(w+5)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}) \\\\ \\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{w-5}-\\frac{4(w-5)(w+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}}{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)}(w+5)}{\\cancel{(w-5)}}-\\frac{4(w-5)\\cancel{(w+5)}}{\\cancel{w+5}} \\\\ 1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5) \\\\ 1 & =w+5-4w+20 \\\\ 3w & =24 \\\\ w & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $8\\ne -5$ and $8\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=8$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=8.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294400 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294587 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1069 "q-276599" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{2}t,\\sqrt{2}t),$ where $t$ is a positive real number. Find the polar coordinates of $P$ expressed in terms of $t.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nExpressed in terms of $t,$ the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1072 #uuid "a8737061-96e6-57b4-a5d5-3ebc97163ea2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294847 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}t}{\\sqrt{2}t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2t,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294200 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294716 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1069 "q-276605" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1072 #uuid "de5f3965-e17e-59a9-a994-292e1fa94acf" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294848 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294034 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294155 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294503 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1069 "q-276607" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{3x-12}\\,?$\n\nThe domain is\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup (${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1072 #uuid "81159dc4-76c4-50b0-ae3d-b2812d3bd662" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294849 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-12 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294086 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294303 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294673 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1069 "q-276609" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{9}{8x-20}\\,?$\n\nThe domain is\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1072 #uuid "a8a0c925-e341-5ee4-9517-5e86e3ff0098" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294850 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-20 & =0 \\\\ 8x & =20 \\\\ x & =\\frac{20}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294606 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294616 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294683 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1069 "q-276611" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{9x+18}-4\\,?$\n\nThe domain is\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup (${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1072 #uuid "e1f22a3e-9ad7-5015-9058-e9b0d141c260" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294851 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x+18 & =0 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{9} \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294204 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294452 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1069 "q-276621" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,0).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1072 #uuid "aceaf5a2-e3e9-5de6-a9c8-0323c48fe731" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294852 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\pi ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294103 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294406 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294744 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1069 "q-276622" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{15x-9}+6\\,?$\n\nThe domain is\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1072 #uuid "cdf34a82-26e8-5a53-9b63-fd836ebd1c41" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294853 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x-9 & =0 \\\\ 15x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{15} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294075 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294142 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1069 "q-276625" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,2).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1072 #uuid "12ac0cb0-d57b-5f92-8718-3db545ebc684" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294854 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294261 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294828 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1069 "q-276627" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-3).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1072 #uuid "a44fb141-35c1-56e8-a035-1f9d9bff6beb" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294855 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(3,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293998 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1069 "q-276641" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1071 "If the domain of the function $q(x)=\\frac{4}{9x-k}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1072 #uuid "40c942eb-8973-5fd4-bf6b-efc2f8533e2d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294856 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-k & =0 \\\\ 9x & =k \\\\ x & =\\frac{k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{k}{9}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{k}{9},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{9} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294201 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1069 "q-276643" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1072 #uuid "0e31c6b4-744c-5f6b-83bb-dc39caa8f302" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294857 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294527 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1069 "q-276658" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+7}{4x-12n}$ is $(-\\infty ,6)\\cup (6,\\infty ),$ then $n=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1072 #uuid "75fd3ec3-8e77-50db-8ff4-14af88b3d719" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294858 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-12n & =0 \\\\ 4x & =12n \\\\ x & =3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=3n.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=6$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $3n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n & =6 \\\\ n & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293957 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1069 "q-276663" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+7}{bx+5}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{6})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ),$ then $b=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1072 #uuid "d2acc18f-6649-5da5-99ec-a581a511537c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294859 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}bx+5 & =0 \\\\ bx & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{5}{b}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-\\frac{5}{6}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{5}{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{b} & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{b} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294818 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1069 "q-276674" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1071 "The multiplicity of the root $x=3$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+11x-6$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1072 #uuid "05e2be1c-f741-5f98-a2e9-33932216702a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294860 1074 "Since $x=3$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $11$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-3}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-3)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294089 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1069 "q-276676" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8x-9}{kx-2}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1072 #uuid "06697c74-28dd-559d-aa6d-4ff4ef877e9b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294861 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2 & =0 \\\\ kx & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{2}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{2}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294046 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294134 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294456 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1069 "q-276679" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{x+3}+2\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup (${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1072 #uuid "ab6ddb06-6de8-57fc-afa4-492eb5c580cb" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294862 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abb045c42cc762e74407610e8a082a7e0adfc89374232a911f974762e5aa2db6.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294002 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294440 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294672 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1069 "q-276682" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3x-5}+\\frac{7}{2}\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup (${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1072 #uuid "be751985-517a-5f9c-aac7-ff655c403681" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294863 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{7}{2},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{7}{2})\\cup (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/03/0369af7512da3b65db27bd9f7945009a3ea15dfbac64cba9cc21adb9c1f19b17.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293940 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294342 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294722 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1069 "q-276683" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2-6x}-2\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1072 #uuid "e06d3b8c-8a4f-54c9-9df5-b202feed1b6d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294864 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27f800d93b5d4c84e98d0b7332388c5b3d513e4b896adabe6e410e4c3e3b684c.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293969 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294511 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294588 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1069 "q-276684" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{12}{7x-8}+\\frac{2}{3}\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1072 #uuid "84e65ece-f611-59bb-b53e-8254e8081352" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294865 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{2}{3},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3})\\cup (\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c218be8082733d6e1026b8d6f810d43ba4ea3e10928f43260a34a1c4cde7f7f6.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293947 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294555 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294711 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1069 "q-276692" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{9}{2x+9}+5\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1072 #uuid "8f1e9d48-4b9a-5415-83bb-4467d8d9a1d3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294866 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=5.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=5,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0da9b33dde51888d770d9e095d74b628698d870727a53f252948d99644c7bc3d.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294162 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294405 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294535 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1069 "q-276693" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{3}{2x-7}+1\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1072 #uuid "7b8d790b-336f-5954-a5a1-72f858559414" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294867 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1a74169e0b8ed0494364e704ec742ed6a399f60db118a9506185c31ee61aaa80.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294101 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294355 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1069 "q-276705" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1071 "![](images/d7/d7f419367bf3a3c1d1f325d179fa126c93c5d63d2df2e1531800085b48228ae3.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1072 #uuid "7f85949a-5e59-59a9-883f-049912293ec4" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294868 1074 "To solve the inequality $p(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n$x\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/da/da89900d3087b93e20a63485b35af41097a2bf21a48f88a027b9afd3c1bb276c.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293943 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294294 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294699 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294792 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1069 "q-276729" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1071 "![](images/8c/8c6e305056cc2128d2dffdbe4ae84cd21d7767dbfce67ca721a94cf24e54420d.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)>0.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃(-2,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)⋃($\n{{field-4}}$,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1072 #uuid "dd045457-92d9-53de-ad04-0578ae8bd621" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294869 1074 "To solve the inequality $p(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\,-\\infty ,-4)⋃(-2,0)⋃(1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n![](images/01/012cbba2f025420a8218565dc3a2877f5e5e9ac4da92f2f1afafe38be5c597ec.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294464 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1069 "q-27673" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1071 "![](images/41/4140c24e9aa2d92d5faea4e1db15022f37fcc390eb183fe6c4c3505ed21941d1.png)\n\nGiven $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AC}?$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1072 #uuid "bc32d1e6-b564-5bbb-817b-1c0b8e2c7172" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294870 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $B$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AC}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below:\n\n![](images/aa/aaf90e409289b16816cb31aeed4461c3e354c07fd946ed44ce73b73b6fd3e4d4.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294274 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294533 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1069 "q-276739" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1071 "Solve the inequality $6-2\\sqrt{x-7}\\le 14.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1072 #uuid "1412a9ac-7937-5fc2-8140-0cfe24515eb3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294871 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 14 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-7} & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-4),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-7\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 7.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294115 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294338 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294610 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1069 "q-276744" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1071 "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6\\ge 2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1072 #uuid "a5cfa7d0-350c-55fc-a9cc-0e6072e071e6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294872 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6 & \\ge 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x} & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+5x} & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(12).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4+5x})}^{2} & \\le {12}^{2} \\\\ 4+5x & \\le 144 \\\\ 5x & \\le 140 \\\\ x & \\le 28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+5x & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x & \\ge -4 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 28,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,28].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{4}{5},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,28]\\cap [-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{4}{5},28]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{4}{5},28],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le 28.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293953 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293962 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294008 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1069 "q-276747" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1071 "Solve the inequality $-{2}^{2x-3}{x}^{2}+3x\\cdot {2}^{2x-3}>-4\\cdot {2}^{2x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}} $<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1072 #uuid "3836ea8d-7800-5dbe-8501-fc6f14327b62" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294873 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{2}^{2x-3}{x}^{2}+3x\\cdot {2}^{2x-3} & >-4\\cdot {2}^{2x-3} \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+3x+4){2}^{2x-3} & >0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-3x-4){2}^{2x-3} & >0 \\\\ -(x+1)(x-4){2}^{2x-3} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${2}^{2x-3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-(x+1)(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThis represents a downwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ The parabola is above the $x$-axis when $-1<x<4.$\n\n![](images/df/df6c1c0ac26d9074f8c726b2265780fefad631561d7a729d2dfc7998ef043859.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-1<x<4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294524 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294583 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294777 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1069 "q-276749" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1071 "Solve the inequality ${3}^{4-5x}x<{3}^{2-5x}{x}^{2}.$\n\n$x<${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1072 #uuid "3d05329a-9380-51b3-8a69-05f2c1b0ff30" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294874 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-5x}x & <{3}^{2-5x}{x}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{4-5x}x-{3}^{2-5x}{x}^{2} & <0 \\\\ ({3}^{2}x-{x}^{2})\\cdot {3}^{2-5x} & <0 \\\\ (9x-{x}^{2})\\cdot {3}^{2-5x} & <0 \\\\ x(9-x)\\cdot {3}^{2-5x} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{2-5x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(9-x)<0.\n$$\n\nThis represents a downwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=9.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when  $x<0$ or $x>9.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c9e8e25c57c88476bedceea237fe78eb2a28d48008493f8609ac31d9aeda30b.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<0$ or $x>9.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294360 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1069 "q-276753" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1071 "Solve for $p$ where $\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}=-\\frac{p-2}{6p}.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1072 #uuid "d80ccd13-b047-5b12-bb12-c0d4a5ea269a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294875 1074 "We note that $p=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $p$ and $6p.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(p,6p)=6p.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6p,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p} & =-\\frac{p-2}{6p} \\\\ 6p\\cdot (\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}) & =6p\\cdot (-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ 6p\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{6p}-6\\cdot p\\cdot \\frac{2}{p} & =6p(-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ \\cancel{6p}\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{\\cancel{6p}}-6\\cdot \\cancel{p}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{p}} & =\\cancel{6p}(-\\frac{p-2}{\\cancel{6p}}) \\\\ p+6-12 & =-(p-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $p,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+6-12 & =-(p-2) \\\\ p-6 & =-p+2 \\\\ 2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $p=0$ as a solution. However, since $p=4\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $p=4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294015 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1069 "q-276759" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1071 "Solve for $d$ where $\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}=1.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1072 #uuid "f9baf8cb-a455-5789-a893-e9234db79276" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294876 1074 "We note that $d=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3d$ and $4d.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3d,4d)=12d.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12d,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =1 \\\\ 12d(\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}) & =12d\\cdot 1 \\\\ 12d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+12d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot 3d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+3\\cdot 4d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{3d}\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{\\cancel{3d}}+3\\cdot \\cancel{4d}\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{\\cancel{4d}} & =12d \\\\ 4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $d,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d \\\\ 40-4d+9d-15 & =12d \\\\ 25+5d & =12d \\\\ -7d & =-25 \\\\ d & =\\frac{25}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $d=0$ as a solution. However, since $d=\\frac{25}{7}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $d=\\frac{25}{7}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294084 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294255 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294275 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294318 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1069 "q-276760" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1071 "![](images/dc/dc1af37c27d53e918ce8bee58789250b3c68833b6fae4c0c3721e4be4aad19f3.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,3]⋃[$\n{{field-4}}$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1072 #uuid "23fa2434-8f68-505a-9665-31eeb79e5208" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294877 1074 "To solve the inequality $p(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in [\\,-3,-1]⋃[1,3]⋃[4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n![](images/77/77f15f6fdfc055595bfff4d946e993c8cccd72f3fcc67e732c7d6b6abde2abec.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294035 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1069 "q-276764" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<2).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1072 #uuid "bf1a4e32-c396-58bc-852e-d3a37e99dd6d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294878 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(Z<\\frac{2-5}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d13179aa11fed4255ce0997c71063a6398fdbb9c6124a4048c9a29759317ab8.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.5)=0.3085.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<2)=0.3085.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294537 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294728 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1069 "q-276774" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1071 "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,{300}^{∘}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1072 #uuid "83820c39-8bef-53bd-a5fc-3568dd433cbb" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294879 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294269 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294528 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1069 "q-276786" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1071 "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1072 #uuid "3bdc99cf-7f58-58bc-8d19-759a3c38b4ba" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294880 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},-4\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294091 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294104 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294484 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294669 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1069 "q-276787" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1071 "Given the polynomial $P(x)=-x(x+3)(x-2)(x-4),$ find the solution of $P(x)<0.$\n\n$x\\in (\\,-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}$,\\,2)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1072 #uuid "8c6d5880-31f2-5070-b358-b35993359243" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294881 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=-x(x+3)(x-2)(x-4)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0,$ $x=2$ and $x=4,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a quartic polynomial with a negative leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/88/889f60e9a8dac71f99dd7e676b77c3e0a1eb7b944305e21b9d4d1b6897dbbb15.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)⋃(0,2)⋃(4,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294301 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294782 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1069 "q-276791" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1071 "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1072 #uuid "bfa9ea2c-dfab-54c2-ba47-4e82fd54912f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294882 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294262 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1069 "q-276793" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{kx-1}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5+x}{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1072 #uuid "424480f5-f543-5930-b060-4a890b9488ad" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294883 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{kx-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{ky-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{ky-1} \\\\ x(ky-1) & =5 \\\\ kxy-x & =5 \\\\ kxy & =5+x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5+x}{kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5+x}{kx}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294287 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294513 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1069 "q-276798" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1071 "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $3,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1072 #uuid "a88a0577-f66a-514f-9dd3-d71688a75644" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294884 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294702 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294776 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1069 "q-276803" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1072 #uuid "60b828e5-8a1d-5f25-a3d7-f526a8ab5604" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294885 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294116 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294531 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1069 "q-276807" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1072 #uuid "18267ca6-6217-5e13-9301-7563b567900e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294886 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294370 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294398 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1069 "q-276838" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1072 #uuid "8de1e1e5-b9e0-5943-ba28-afbdac6dbd1d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294887 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294138 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1069 "q-276856" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1071 "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}=\\frac{5}{12m}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1072 #uuid "e4ecd1e1-26ef-517b-818e-608c9a4f622b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294888 1074 "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $m,\\,6m,$ and $12m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,6m,12m)=12m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m} & =\\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12m(\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}) & =12m(\\frac{5}{12m}) \\\\ 12m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+12m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+2\\cdot 6m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{\\cancel{6m}} & =\\cancel{12m}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{12m}} \\\\ 36+2(4+m) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36+2(4+m) & =5 \\\\ 36+8+2m & =5 \\\\ 44+2m & =5 \\\\ 2m & =-39 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. However, since $m=-\\frac{39}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{39}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294388 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1069 "q-276861" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1071 "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}=\\frac{7}{10a}.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1072 #uuid "78703a9d-2e58-5cc3-af40-d56bda7e72a7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294889 1074 "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a,\\,5a,$ and $10a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,5a,10a)=10a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a} & =\\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10a(\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}) & =10a(\\frac{7}{10a}) \\\\ 10a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+10a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+2\\cdot 5a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{\\cancel{a}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{5a}\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{\\cancel{5a}} & =\\cancel{10a}\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{10a}} \\\\ 10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7 \\\\ 10a+50+4-2a & =7 \\\\ 8a+54 & =7 \\\\ 8a & =-47 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{47}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. However, since $a=-\\frac{47}{8}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=-\\frac{47}{8}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294817 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1069 "q-276864" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1071 "Solve for $f$ where $\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}=\\frac{f+2}{2f}.$\n\n$f=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1072 #uuid "8ce28150-db62-5cfe-975f-e10652804e6e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294890 1074 "We note that $f=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $f,\\,2f,$ and $5f.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(f,2f,5f)=10f.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10f,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =\\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10f(\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}) & =10f(\\frac{f+2}{2f}) \\\\ 10f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-10f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =10f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-2\\cdot 5f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =5\\cdot 2f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{f}\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{\\cancel{f}}-2\\cdot \\cancel{5f}\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{\\cancel{5f}} & =5\\cdot \\cancel{2f}\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{\\cancel{2f}} \\\\ 10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $f,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2) \\\\ 10f+60-6f-18 & =5f+10 \\\\ 4f+42 & =5f+10 \\\\ -f & =-32 \\\\ f & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $f=0$ as a solution. But since $f=32\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $f=32.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293952 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1069 "q-276866" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1071 "Solve for $y$ where $\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}=\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1072 #uuid "bec5822f-a63b-51ac-9376-ed1a887e1747" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294891 1074 "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $4y,\\,{y}^{2},$ and $2{y}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4y,{y}^{2},2{y}^{2})=4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y} & =\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}(\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}) & =4{y}^{2}(\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4y\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =2\\cdot 2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}-\\cancel{4y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4y}} & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{2{y}^{2}}} \\\\ 4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 4y+8-5y & =6 \\\\ -y+8 & =6 \\\\ -y & =-2 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294203 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1069 "q-276874" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1071 "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1072 #uuid "98fbfe7e-e55e-51d1-bbb6-68913fe5f605" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294892 1074 "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $n$ and ${n}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n,{n}^{2})={n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}(\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}) & ={n}^{2}(\\frac{6}{n}) \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & ={n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =n\\cdot n\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}+\\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}} & =n\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6 \\\\ 2n+2 & =6n \\\\ -4n & =-2 \\\\ n & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294179 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294675 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294739 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1069 "q-276950" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9).$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\le \\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1072 #uuid "771eaa44-b3e5-5365-b2c4-cde4e6dfbce5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294893 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)}\\cdot {10}^{1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ (2-x)\\cdot 10 & \\le x+9 \\\\ 20-10x & \\le x+9 \\\\ -11x & \\le -11 \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2 \\\\ x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 1,$ which can be expressed as $[1,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<2$ and $x>-11,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(-11,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[1,\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,2)\\cap (-9,\\infty )=[1,2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [1,2),$ which can also be written as $1\\le x<2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294184 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1069 "q-276993" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the first term is $15$ and the common ratio is $6?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1072 #uuid "2eec7bb0-df76-5715-b350-2b2a01a3c6a8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294894 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=15$ and $r=6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{15(1-{6}^{N})}{1-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{5}(1-{6}^{N}) \\\\  & =3({6}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293999 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294167 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1069 "q-277012" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1071 "![](images/d9/d931b377a8d62bf3b56672a0f556c0581e8400c5b1c7b1f783f08e4297342855.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={210}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1072 #uuid "3c0494bb-9b54-5cfd-9186-bd801e2eaf2d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294895 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{210}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294725 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294789 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1069 "q-277027" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1071 "![](images/db/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1072 #uuid "fb90e8f9-7bb2-5ebe-9317-d50976c6167d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294896 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294653 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1069 "q-277042" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1071 "![](images/24/241d4b1d29efddc5a9feb13b835d0caab6af354d3f62387dc92d14ff81942a6d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{RP}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{RP}=$   \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1072 #uuid "96672b8f-0df7-5659-b64b-ee16448cf9a3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294897 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $R$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RP}=\\overset{\\to }{RT}+\\overset{\\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b4ebdca78235f8f82564db1821320c7bb886447cb012813173e9c50558dbc9bb.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/54/540f94306cc497d87dc1f15d61ea929e042944f7c6f711a2bcf6b1f944c48228.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RP} & =\\overset{\\to }{RT}+\\overset{\\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP} \\\\  & =d-c+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294055 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294747 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1069 "q-277162" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1071 "For which values of $x$ is $2{x}^{2}-10x\\le 0?$\n\n$x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1072 #uuid "7ac14dee-d448-542b-80f4-f2d79697416e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294898 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-10x & \\le 0 \\\\ 2x(x-5) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $2x(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $2x(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $2x(x-5)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $2x(x-5)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0,5).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=0$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $2{x}^{2}-10x\\le 0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in [0,5].\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294045 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294609 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1069 "q-277163" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+x-30<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1072 #uuid "c75b2698-0506-52be-99c0-bd84e19ba874" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294899 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-30 & <0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-5) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-5)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-5)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+x-30<0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-6,5).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294676 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294705 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1069 "q-277165" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+16x+20<3x-22?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1072 #uuid "358763b3-8edb-5572-81a0-3e5c7271f5fd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294900 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+20 & <3x-22 \\\\ {x}^{2}+13x+42 & <0 \\\\ (x+7)(x+6) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x+6)=0.$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=-6,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,-6),$ and $(-6,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,-6)$ | $(-6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x+6)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x+6)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x+6)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-7,-6).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+16x+20<3x-22$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-7,-6).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294539 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294586 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294735 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1069 "q-277167" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-2x-8\\ge 0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]\\cup [$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1072 #uuid "2fe79fd6-5698-5061-8fea-cdf6cb1d40c0" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294901 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-4) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-4)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-2$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-2x-8\\ge 0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [4,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294030 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294417 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294665 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1069 "q-277184" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-3x>0.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}} $x<$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1072 #uuid "b95c6841-0eb4-5708-9f06-d656fee913d4" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294902 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-3x>0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ -x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/27/270898d7313f695f3b879b461942f6b653362760b565cc3edfddc58b1de6d1ec.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the $x$-axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-3<x<0.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294536 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294660 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1069 "q-277200" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1072 #uuid "69b56d75-ccf1-51bc-b43e-408c64b94822" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294903 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294623 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1069 "q-277229" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{2x+10}-4.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1072 #uuid "0ee58c17-51a1-5285-993c-af2e17bcc9ac" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294904 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2x+10}-4=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2x+10}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2x+10} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2x+10})^{3} & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 2x+10 & =64 \\\\ 2x & =54 \\\\ x & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=27.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294466 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1069 "q-277244" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=9\\sqrt[7]{2x-6}-9.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1072 #uuid "b5f5bbe1-b914-584a-afff-a9643199268b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294905 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n9\\sqrt[7]{2x-6}-9=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\sqrt[7]{2x-6}-9 & =0 \\\\ 9\\sqrt[7]{2x-6} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{2x-6} & =1 \\\\ (\\sqrt[7]{2x-6})^{7} & ={1}^{7} \\\\ 2x-6 & =1 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294183 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1069 "q-277246" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=-\\sqrt[9]{9-12x}-1.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1072 #uuid "8029a731-e976-548c-93ae-f2a55242e3da" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294906 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt[9]{9-12x}-1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt[9]{9-12x}-1 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt[9]{9-12x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt[9]{9-12x} & =-1 \\\\ (\\sqrt[9]{9-12x})^{9} & =(-1)^{9} \\\\ 9-12x & =-1 \\\\ -12x & =-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{6}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294724 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1069 "q-277256" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=-5\\sqrt[8]{4x+9}+5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1072 #uuid "01b3f8e0-559d-5f61-a595-04b8c05b49b7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294907 1074 "To find the roots of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-5\\sqrt[8]{4x+9}+5=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\sqrt[8]{4x+9}+5 & =0 \\\\ -5\\sqrt[8]{4x+9} & =-5 \\\\ \\sqrt[8]{4x+9} & =1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt[8]{4x+9})}^{8} & ={1}^{8} \\\\ 4x+9 & =1 \\\\ 4x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\sqrt[8]{4\\cdot (-2)+9}+5 & =0 \\\\ -5\\sqrt[8]{1}+5 & =0 \\\\ -5\\cdot 1+5 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294277 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294631 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294783 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1069 "q-277261" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+6x\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\le $\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1072 #uuid "1971e49b-6cb6-5f2b-b450-006719f9fc3a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294908 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ x(x+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/7d/7d9fd824b1e4d63e417e002c07a01adb4c14f73fc81be66c31a5c9aa30ab7ffd.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 0.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294067 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294366 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294666 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1069 "q-277263" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-7x\\le 0.$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1072 #uuid "60895938-ad1c-5107-a820-4aa07d0eeda7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294909 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-7x\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ x(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=7,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/e7/e7f9e5a051acca0be45340695fd6c2ddcb9d47936cc1f713245bd22d33151c97.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $0\\le x\\le 7.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294227 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294267 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294389 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1069 "q-277266" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-8x+12<0.$\n\n{{field-1}} $<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1072 #uuid "4881375e-7b7c-5819-a1a4-5f647749620a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294910 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-8x+12\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d35f232f4a0ff7dc26238a1ac75ec8c2dc7216efa75f767dbbbac271b8a23a79.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2<x<6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294005 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294245 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294265 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1069 "q-277268" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-5x<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$\\,\\text{or}\\,x>$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1072 #uuid "325f9677-ba8d-5745-8c7d-4ef2f1129640" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294911 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-5x<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-5x\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ -x(x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/19/196587385ffa960d79d64d66f6ba14481905dd3de6159ecaec6a8e7bd2ab58b8.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or $x>0.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294460 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294734 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1069 "q-277383" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1071 "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2$ and the line $y=4.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1072 #uuid "a92b50ba-f7e2-5e5c-b9d8-d78606f50367" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294912 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ 5 & =6x-4 \\\\ 9 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\frac{3}{2},4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f07fc53462af6fbb7de0a901ae23721a2eb84f6e83bd5577a2659c175519fc67.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294525 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294796 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1069 "q-277386" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1071 "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{2x+3}$ and the line $y=1.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1072 #uuid "2ae4c495-54ef-52db-9fdb-26db2eb1792b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294913 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{2x+3} \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2x+3} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =2x+3 \\\\ -1 & =2x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/19/192f47788ac2bc716c4f3e0d8fa9efe9b2c26dd9e32bb9296cfcb00328e5db05.png)" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294809 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1069 "q-277432" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1071 "The line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1072 #uuid "3d1587a6-162e-53bd-a22e-862be68f17eb" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294914 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-k \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-k & =-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (4x-k)(x+1) & =-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x-kx-k+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+(4-k)x+(2-k) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(4-k)\\pm \\sqrt{(4-k)^{2}-4(4)(2-k)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{16-8k+{k}^{2}-32+16k}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+8k-16}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+8k-16 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{(8)^{2}-4(1)(-16)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $k=-4+4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293955 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293987 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1069 "q-277446" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\ge 5x+14$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]\\,⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1072 #uuid "c958b5aa-40c9-5af9-9fcd-0014c93ed3e8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294915 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 5x+14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-14 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x-14$ lies **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-14 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=7,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/62/628325ae599d6b004fdabe85319ec9f8acd701c76b776cc4dea6b321cb554415.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5x-14\\ge 0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 7.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]⋃[7,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294381 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294551 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294690 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1069 "q-277664" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}>12x$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,⋃\\,(${{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1072 #uuid "51c36791-884d-5275-b991-831465b1110a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294916 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >12x \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-12x$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ x(x-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=12,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387b02ad3416b06b3f86f12c99b921800ab4b86f39be9da0fc48883952d366d2.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<0$ or $x>12.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)⋃(12,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293973 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294659 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294764 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1069 "q-277667" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1071 "Solve the inequality $4{x}^{2}-17x>-2{x}^{2}+45$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,⋃\\,(${{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1072 #uuid "3a2a4516-1108-5dd6-a560-cbcd3875ee84" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294917 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-17x & >-2{x}^{2}+45 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=6{x}^{2}-17x-45$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-27x)+(10x-45) & =0 \\\\ 3x(2x-9)+5(2x-9) & =0 \\\\ (3x+5)(2x-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f73c9906e6b65d1ddbda91619c7ffbb77408faa0541e2bc16565e0127d334312.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-45>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<-\\frac{5}{3}$ or $x>\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})⋃(\\frac{9}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294346 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294750 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1069 "q-277670" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1071 "Solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}>-x-12$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1072 #uuid "05f5e360-8ecb-5568-bf13-5ca7a9c30286" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294918 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & >-x-12 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+x+12 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x+3)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32e9a7babfbe9132a3a3e18c2f1efcbc3a33cd59bccc02099c7045b3fc39ee75.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+x+12>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3<x<4.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-3,4).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294489 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294719 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1069 "q-277671" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2{x}^{2}>10x+12$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1072 #uuid "599fe53d-bb98-565d-9430-dc731e24c6f5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294919 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2} & >10x+12 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-10x-12 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}-10x-12$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-10x-12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+6x)+(4x+12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+3)+4(x+3) & =0 \\\\ (2x+4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=-2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/53/539e0e68f4a03c0bd4a309ebf2c4a00638d99c6eee233ca8f2cef3814e419c95.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}-10x-12>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3<x<-2.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-3,-2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294127 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294352 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294720 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1069 "q-277771" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1071 "Construct the matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T$ that acts on a vector in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- It rotates the vector by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin, then\n\n- stretches the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- shears the result with a shear factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1072 #uuid "61d89e52-8793-52b7-9d83-7ebf0e38a50e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294920 1074 "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the shearing. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =RQP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294139 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294760 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1069 "q-277798" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1071 "Solve the inequality $2+\\sqrt[3]{7x+6}<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1072 #uuid "168964c8-87be-53e3-966d-7f41ef0eb000" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294921 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt[3]{7x+6} & <0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{7x+6} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{7x+6})}^{3} & <(-2)^{3} \\\\ 7x+6 & <-8 \\\\ 7x & <-14 \\\\ x & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294081 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294279 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1069 "q-277801" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1071 "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1072 #uuid "de2dc34f-1c0d-5c73-aef7-be1610303590" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294922 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(1,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293991 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294043 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294102 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294390 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1069 "q-277802" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1071 "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=36$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1072 #uuid "adc603df-096b-5ec7-a1ef-a0ecd627217a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294923 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-4(0)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294433 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294759 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1069 "q-277805" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=8$ intersects the line $y=x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1072 #uuid "29284243-d0bd-50b3-bdc9-168339e98a71" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294924 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294047 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1069 "q-277809" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1071 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1072 #uuid "7a4d1c45-0cb2-5b73-8648-4c82b318de63" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294925 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294401 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1069 "q-277819" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1071 "Expressed in radians, $4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1072 #uuid "67417992-a0a5-5f1e-80ca-97d654c4f2a0" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294926 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=4(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294096 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294684 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1069 "q-277827" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1071 "Solve the inequality $6-\\sqrt[3]{10+3x}<2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1072 #uuid "6a04d7f6-a54f-5f3f-be96-980af102d002" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294927 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & <2 \\\\ -\\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & <-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{10+3x})}^{3} & >{4}^{3} \\\\ 10+3x & >64 \\\\ 3x & >54 \\\\ x & >18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294465 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294784 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1069 "q-277833" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2}+8<10.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1072 #uuid "4eaff4cd-df2e-59db-8b03-5f1d524a7e1e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294928 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2}+8 & <10 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2} & <2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6x-2} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{6x-2})}^{3} & <{4}^{3} \\\\ 6x-2 & <64 \\\\ 6x & <66 \\\\ x & <11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294080 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294833 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1069 "q-277834" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1071 "Solve the inequality $2-\\sqrt{6x-2}<10.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1072 #uuid "3eace645-290a-56c6-9aa1-81df2f7c07d5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294929 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{6x-2} & <10 \\\\ -\\sqrt{6x-2} & <8 \\\\ \\sqrt{6x-2} & >-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-8),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n6x-2\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294226 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294797 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1069 "q-277839" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{5-10x}+2>-7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1072 #uuid "55fa1485-3408-55a9-8852-2e0ee1cc068a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294930 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-10x}+2 & >-7 \\\\ \\sqrt{5-10x} & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-3),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-10x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -10x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294300 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294581 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1069 "q-277844" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1}\\ge 2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1072 #uuid "5e7b48d4-ce79-57fa-8f7a-79e784ea9598" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294931 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 2 \\\\ {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $3$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2x-1}) & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x-1 & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}({log}_{3}⁡(8)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293995 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294078 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1069 "q-277846" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x}>7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1072 #uuid "6d5edd14-7e7b-53cf-8d2d-da6793a07e0a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294932 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >7 \\\\ {4}^{x} & >14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $4$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x}) & >{log}_{4}⁡(14) \\\\ x & >{log}_{4}⁡(14).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294042 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294296 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1069 "q-277847" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1071 "Solve the inequality $8\\cdot {9}^{x}\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1072 #uuid "afa06374-fd1e-58d7-85c5-0dcba6a7a370" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294933 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $9$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡(\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡({12}^{-1}) \\\\ x & \\ge -{log}_{9}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294157 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294446 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1069 "q-277853" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}}<-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1072 #uuid "448eec0f-6fda-5857-9f85-a54b2dea40a1" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294934 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-9 \\\\ {e}^{2-3x} & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-3x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 2-3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ -3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2 \\\\ 3x & <2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294232 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1069 "q-277872" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $7$th term is $512$ and the common ratio is $2?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1072 #uuid "69a2d98f-a3eb-55a5-9479-4dc1b59fa42b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294935 1074 "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 512 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{(7-1)} \\\\ 512 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=8$ and $r=2.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{8(1-{2}^{N})}{1-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1-{2}^{N})}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-8(1-{2}^{N}) \\\\  & =8({2}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294069 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294215 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294408 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1069 "q-277887" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1071 "Solve the inequality $-(0.75)^{2-3x}<(0.98)^{3x-1}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1072 #uuid "da4d073a-f234-5a95-bce4-b5a820095246" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294936 1074 "Since $(0.98)^{3x-1}$ is always positive and $-(0.75)^{2-3x}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-(0.75)^{2-3x}<(0.98)^{3x-1}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294039 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294620 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1069 "q-277888" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1071 "Solve the inequality $-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{3-4x}<{8}^{3-x}.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\,<\\,\\infty $" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1072 #uuid "71baa8b8-3f83-5969-8e10-87c8953b0f07" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294937 1074 "Since ${8}^{3-x}$ is always positive and $-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{3-4x}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{3-4x}<{8}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294473 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1069 "q-277896" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1072 #uuid "64dd2723-00ff-5d92-8485-1c9558f6728a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294938 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $6,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{6}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{6}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{6+9}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294812 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1069 "q-277901" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $14$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1072 #uuid "9c32f440-b745-5987-a016-950ee37db448" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294939 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $14,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{14}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{14}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{14}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{14+8}={x}^{22}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $22.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294437 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1069 "q-277910" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1072 #uuid "1b1b3a3e-4594-5dcf-9cc0-0bf33b63dbf9" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294940 1074 "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=4{x}^{3}$ and $Q=4{x}^{3}-10.$ Then $P-Q=10,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294545 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1069 "q-277921" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1071 "![](images/ab/ab3d1fefcefa12133ec4ea7e3e6ce744c88f5a9b25029c950254706097cb4c4d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1072 #uuid "5ee547bf-f7e1-51f3-bed2-e58fc1c418f5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294941 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294022 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294290 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1069 "q-277923" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1071 "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1072 #uuid "5b7e4e8f-cd81-5ee3-ac55-a56d0433ac6d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294942 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=64$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=64,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{64}=8.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=8.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 8=16.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294243 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1069 "q-277931" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1072 #uuid "29e6ffa8-9f33-56f5-810e-d7c6edc1eff6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294943 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294508 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1069 "q-277935" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds  $30$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,\\frac{8}{3},\\,\\frac{32}{9},\\,\\frac{128}{27},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $30$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1072 #uuid "35b2715b-e1e7-51f9-aae1-7cff0716190b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294944 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{\\frac{8}{3}}{2}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $30.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >30 \\\\ 2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >30 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >15 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1≈10.413.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294648 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1069 "q-277944" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1072 #uuid "b455ef9d-70d7-5e21-8d18-b8f95a10e8e0" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294945 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-8}{2+0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294344 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1069 "q-277946" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1072 #uuid "de7a55d7-6295-54ce-a27e-c5177af89309" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294946 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{x},$ $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293982 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1069 "q-277950" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+6x}{({x}^{2}-2x)(1-2{x}^{2})}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1072 #uuid "d77c2a09-e11a-5b23-914b-66260dcc1d34" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294947 1074 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{4}{x},$ $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{8-0}{-2+0+0-0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294333 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1069 "q-277964" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds  $9\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,6\\sqrt{7},\\,84,\\,168\\sqrt{7},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $9\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1072 #uuid "a7cb0613-cbab-5fc5-a24e-b4cca5e60779" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294948 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{6\\sqrt{7}}{3}=2\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $9\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >9\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >9\\,000 \\\\ {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >3\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1≈5.805.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $9\\,000.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294131 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1069 "q-277971" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,25,\\,5,\\,1,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1072 #uuid "bdd1e205-0b3b-52a5-a62b-31454ee6e551" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294949 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{25}{125}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={5}^{4-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,31.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ {5}^{4-n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{4-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ (4-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ 4-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-4 \\\\ n & >4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}≈9.020.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,31.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294515 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294538 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1069 "q-278048" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $(x-6)^{3}+5\\le 0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1072 #uuid "f3c4d2ff-ba9b-5221-b985-32c4247d60c2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294950 1074 "Isolating the cubic-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6)^{3}+5 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x-6)^{3} & \\le -5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the third root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(x-6)^{3}} & \\le \\sqrt[3]{-5} \\\\ x-6 & \\le \\sqrt[3]{-5} \\\\ x & \\le \\sqrt[3]{-5}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294311 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294704 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1069 "q-278051" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $(x+5)^{7}-1\\ge 8.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1072 #uuid "57b8d0f5-f04b-5c9b-997b-14609e8dde0a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294951 1074 "Isolating the seventh-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{7}-1 & \\ge 8 \\\\ (x+5)^{7} & \\ge 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the seventh root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[7]{(x+5)^{7}} & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{9} \\\\ x+5 & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{9} \\\\ x & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{9}-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294688 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1069 "q-278053" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.004$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n20,\\,4,\\,\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{4}{25},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.004$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1072 #uuid "7822a3f9-ab75-5ede-9c75-b7c13e8e9b8e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294952 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=20$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.004.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.004 \\\\ 20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.004 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.004}{20} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1≈6.292.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.004.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294395 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1069 "q-278078" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1071 "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $972$, and the common ratio is $3.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $30\\,000$ is the{{field-1}}th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1072 #uuid "f1e07266-5cb5-5242-898f-83eaf10c3a32" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294953 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=972$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 972 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 972 & =243{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{972}{243} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $30\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >30\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >7\\,500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈9.122.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30\\,000.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294181 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1069 "q-278083" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1071 "The $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $81$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1072 #uuid "5e93b407-75fa-54c2-b3ba-dd927186be32" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294954 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{5}=81$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{81}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >625 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈16.877.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $17$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294661 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1069 "q-278089" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1071 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $2\\,304$, and the $6$th term is $36\\,864.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $496\\,800$ is the{{field-1}} th term." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1072 #uuid "5566e2a7-47e4-58fa-9f0d-4eef1047eb3d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294955 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and ${a}_{6}=36\\,864$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{36\\,864}{2\\,304} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and  $r=4$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{4-1} \\\\ 2\\,304 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,304 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =36\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $496\\,800.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >496\\,800 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >13\\,800 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈7.876.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $496\\,800.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293997 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294163 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294291 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294611 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1069 "q-278108" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-11\\ge 0$ using the absolute value method.\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1072 #uuid "a5cbf519-73d9-5f30-8098-6519fc130e09" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294956 1074 "Bringing the constant term to the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-11 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 11 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{11} \\\\ |x| & \\ge \\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nTherefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{11}]\\cup [\\sqrt{11},\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294480 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294627 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1069 "q-278121" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $(3-x)^{7}-8\\ge 5.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1072 #uuid "b1eb2c74-5dac-548b-bceb-1b8ee181b495" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294957 1074 "Isolating the seventh-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-x)^{7}-8 & \\ge 5 \\\\ (3-x)^{7} & \\ge 13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the seventh root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[7]{(3-x)^{7}} & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{13} \\\\ 3-x & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{13} \\\\ -x & \\ge \\sqrt[7]{13}-3 \\\\ x & \\le -\\sqrt[7]{13}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294117 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294572 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294579 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1069 "q-278128" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $(x-6)^{2}+4\\ge 11.$\n\n$x\\le ${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1072 #uuid "df9c903f-97b0-52f6-94bf-4203e162ced8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294958 1074 "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6)^{2}+4 & \\ge 11 \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & \\ge 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{7} \\\\ |x-6| & \\ge \\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx-6\\ge \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{or}\\,x-6\\le -\\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6 & \\ge \\sqrt{7} &  &  & x-6 & \\le -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & \\ge \\sqrt{7}+6 &  &  & x & \\le -\\sqrt{7}+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{7}+6\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{7}+6.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294332 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294573 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294825 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1069 "q-278129" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $3(x-5)^{4}-4\\ge 23.$\n\n$x\\le ${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1072 #uuid "39733ec9-fb86-5c5f-a786-f648cefa2ac3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294959 1074 "Isolating the quartic-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x-5)^{4}-4 & \\ge 23 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{4} & \\ge 27 \\\\ (x-5)^{4} & \\ge 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{(x-5)^{4}} & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ |x-5| & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx-5\\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}\\,\\text{or}\\,x-5\\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9} &  &  & x-5 & \\le -\\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}+5 &  &  & x & \\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}+5\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}+5.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294445 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294459 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294576 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294591 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1069 "q-278137" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1071 "Solve $36{x}^{2}>49.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1072 #uuid "f595e243-45f5-5e54-87ed-11d62a125eb0" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294960 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](images/14/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293975 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294663 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294793 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1069 "q-278138" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $(4-x)^{2}-10\\ge 7.$\n\n$x\\le ${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1072 #uuid "b0fa5545-92d1-5f79-993b-a3e1853a3c12" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294961 1074 "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)^{2}-10 & \\ge 7 \\\\ (4-x)^{2} & \\ge 17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(4-x)^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{17} \\\\ |4-x| & \\ge \\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n4-x\\le -\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{or}\\,4-x\\ge \\sqrt{17}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & \\le -\\sqrt{17} &  &  & 4-x & \\ge \\sqrt{17} \\\\ -x & \\le -\\sqrt{17}-4 &  &  & -x & \\ge \\sqrt{17}-4 \\\\ x & \\ge 4+\\sqrt{17} &  &  & x & \\le 4-\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le 4-\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4+\\sqrt{17}.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294283 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294790 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1069 "q-278143" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1071 "Find the exact solution of the inequality $-4(5-x)^{6}+147>-109.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1072 #uuid "2c7a957d-5103-501f-bb6e-32d90b82a9a5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294962 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(5-x)^{6}+147 & >-109 \\\\ -4(5-x)^{6} & >-256 \\\\ (5-x)^{6} & <64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the inequality when we divided both sides by $-4.$\n\nNow, we take the sixth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{(5-x)^{6}} & <\\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ |5-x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-2<5-x<2\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2<5-x & <2 \\\\ -7<-x & <-3 \\\\ 7>x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the lower number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3<x<7\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294017 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294304 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294801 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1069 "q-278181" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1071 "Solve the inequality $({x}^{2}-25)^{3}\\cdot {2}^{3x}<0.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$ \n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1072 #uuid "0a1862c4-03c7-52a6-8964-de3265c18925" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294963 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by ${2}^{3x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25)^{3}\\cdot {2}^{3x} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{({x}^{2}-25)^{3}\\cdot {2}^{3x}}{{2}^{3x}} & <\\frac{0}{{2}^{3x}} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-25)^{3} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-25)^{3}} & <\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ {x}^{2}-25 & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <25 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{25} \\\\ |x| & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $-5<x<5.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293994 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294455 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294761 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294814 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1069 "q-278199" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1071 "Solve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2\\ge 1.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1072 #uuid "6c22c933-4191-53ca-a9de-011c17b8b80e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294964 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2 & \\ge 1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 1+2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{18} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution set is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}\\ge 18$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-18\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-18\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{18})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3\\sqrt{2})(x+3\\sqrt{2}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhence $x=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-18$   is positive, the parabola  opens upward.  Therefore, the solution set to the  given inequality ($y\\ge 0$) lies outside the roots (and  includes them):\n\n![](images/72/72488926dd64954e2c1070a94625ebc91c1c729d70bbf7c5f098364e651efec5.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294335 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294567 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1069 "q-278213" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1071 "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{3x+6}+11\\ge 5.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1072 #uuid "68ebd41a-8ae9-54df-95bb-b51c190c1de8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294965 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3x+6}+11 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3x+6} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+6} & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-3),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n3x+6\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294313 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294626 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294654 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1069 "q-278219" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1071 "Solve the inequality $(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\le ${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1072 #uuid "09dafc94-51dd-50c5-beff-efeb5e675a24" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294966 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by ${6}^{3x-2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}}{{6}^{3x-2}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{6}^{3x-2}} \\\\ (49-{x}^{2})^{3} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(49-{x}^{2})^{3}} & \\le \\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ 49-{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & \\le -49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 49 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{49} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign again of the inequality since we divided by a negative number ($-1$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -7$ or $x\\ge 7.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294253 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294741 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1069 "q-278230" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1071 "Solve the inequality $5x\\cdot {3}^{x+1}+{3}^{x+1}\\ge -6\\cdot {3}^{x+1}.$\n\n$x$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1072 #uuid "57e6ad7d-6548-5f76-972a-8ac3b118ed0f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294967 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\cdot {3}^{x+1}+{3}^{x+1} & \\ge -6\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 5x\\cdot {3}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {3}^{x+1}+{3}^{x+1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (5x+6+1){3}^{x+1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (5x+7){3}^{x+1} & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{x+1}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5x+7){3}^{x+1}}{{3}^{x+1}} & <\\frac{0}{{3}^{x+1}} \\\\ 5x+7 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x & \\ge 0-7 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality above is $x\\ge -\\frac{7}{5}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293942 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294605 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294823 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1069 "q-278239" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1071 "Solve the inequality $-10\\cdot {5}^{2-7x}+3x\\cdot {5}^{2-7x}>-{5}^{2-7x}{x}^{2}.$\n\n$x<${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1072 #uuid "6e062e70-7d42-5d11-afd1-0b9180270e33" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294968 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10\\cdot {5}^{2-7x}+3x\\cdot {5}^{2-7x} & >-{5}^{2-7x}{x}^{2} \\\\ {5}^{2-7x}{x}^{2}-10\\cdot {5}^{2-7x}+3x\\cdot {5}^{2-7x} & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+3x-10){5}^{2-7x} & >0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2){5}^{2-7x} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${5}^{2-7x}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)(x-2){5}^{2-7x}}{{5}^{2-7x}} & >\\frac{0}{{5}^{2-7x}} \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-5$ and $x=2.$ The parabola is above the $x$-axis when $x<-5$ or $x>2.$\n\n![](images/6b/6b72efca34700ab06986460d4d190e0e83a4f0068c9effe20af622082a92b9a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or $x>2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294534 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1069 "q-278251" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1071 "![](images/6b/6bb7a8dc9b34c03e5456b84b843c8975aac8ec8f09135fe9069693febc24ba78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1072 #uuid "769c3ba1-be44-5d72-ab21-003c024cdeed" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294969 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294529 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1069 "q-278254" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1071 "![](images/65/65a76a438b3459a92db0e00b0c9bd281b8a9c58fa68afd2d8e6468a1d99b1cf9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1072 #uuid "c1f1fe35-990c-50d4-ada2-aab504633e3e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294970 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293945 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1069 "q-278255" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1071 "![](images/4c/4c95243ea94951012ec14c6f16b4276a591c10c22798efc82f73bcd9d45e65ae.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1072 #uuid "a9f27b41-29b4-5ec4-8310-cd993b30c28c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294971 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294082 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294152 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1069 "q-278263" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1071 "![](images/c2/c26ec7e1fccc5ada4c43eef55faf9e4fa3e17ed896b1546f472edf744404da1a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=2:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1072 #uuid "02a8ac13-d9fd-5011-b248-20d1e825aecc" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294972 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=2:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{2}{5}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{2}{5}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{2}{5}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}d+\\frac{2}{5}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294174 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294448 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1069 "q-278266" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+5x<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1072 #uuid "a57d75c5-f24e-5db3-a88d-ec73ba8dd6b5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294973 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x & <0 \\\\ x(x+5) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+5)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+5)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-5,0).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+5x<0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-5,0).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294561 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294802 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1069 "q-278271" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{4x-1}\\le {4}^{4x+4}.$\n\n$x$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1072 #uuid "f254d752-eb39-51c2-901d-dca78f8bf02b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294974 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{4x-1} & \\le {4}^{4x+4} \\\\ {x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{4x-1}-{4}^{4x+4} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{4x-1}-{4}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{4x-1} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{5}-{4}^{5})\\cdot {4}^{4x-1} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${4}^{4x-1}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{5}-{4}^{5})\\cdot {4}^{4x-1}}{{4}^{4x-1}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{4}^{4x-1}} \\\\ ({x}^{5}-{4}^{5}) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{5}-{4}^{5}) & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5} & \\le {4}^{5} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{5}} & \\le \\sqrt[5]{{4}^{5}} \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality above is $x\\le 4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294336 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294439 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294601 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1069 "q-278274" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}\\le {9}^{2x+1}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\le x$ \n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1072 #uuid "8bbb7ba0-1fbd-52a3-a62d-8ca79afc6e8c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294975 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le {9}^{2x+1} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}-{9}^{2x+1} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}-{9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{9}^{4})\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${9}^{2x-3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-{9}^{4})\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}}{{9}^{2x-3}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{9}^{2x-3}} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{9}^{4} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{9}^{4} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\le {9}^{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{{9}^{4}} \\\\ |x| & \\le {9}^{2} \\\\ |x| & \\le 81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $-81\\le x<81.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294612 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1069 "q-278280" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1071 "Solve the inequality $3+2\\sqrt{4x+1}>7.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1072 #uuid "f7d6efa8-c53e-51cd-a6ee-3e65231d4543" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294976 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >7 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+1} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+1})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ 4x+1 & >4 \\\\ 4x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )=(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{3}{4}<x.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294266 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294268 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294365 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1069 "q-278282" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294977 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}\\