1069 "q-278306" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294982 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+4x+3>0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cup ($\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294982 1072 #uuid "f1e98bc7-e95b-52e2-a357-9535be5337e5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294982 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+3 & >0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+1) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+3)(x+1)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=-1,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,-1),$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+3)(x+1)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+3)(x+1)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+3)(x+1)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+4x+3>0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293967 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1069 "q-278312" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1071 "![](images/04/0405790e07a49ddb62c4444ba13669ee74e6b7c37d5c2949a244246b8d54b65a.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1072 #uuid "d9d9ea32-678d-544b-b9a2-519e184bd39b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294983 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(5,2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294297 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294820 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1069 "q-278314" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1072 #uuid "fa3f7e29-55cf-509c-9d43-7250db8bff71" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294984 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-3 & <0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-3)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,3).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294020 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294315 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1069 "q-278315" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1071 "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1072 #uuid "3ceddd71-a80c-579b-ab0c-9f93a200bfcd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294985 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=3$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(3,2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294270 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1069 "q-27832" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1071 "![](images/66/6665784a8ce048fd26fbd97038346e8e6fdf6800d645ea0488f1c18986f5864b.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $△ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ If $m∠BAC={110}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠HCA.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1072 #uuid "94753d7f-95e4-5b39-9c21-ede7ecea7547" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294986 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HAC+m∠BAC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC+{110}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△ACH$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HCA+m∠HAC+m∠AHC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{70}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{160}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294689 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1069 "q-278323" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1071 "The multiplicity of the root $x=-6$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+60x+72$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1072 #uuid "449b47d2-e5c5-5d76-b426-0416a0bd90dc" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294987 1074 "Since $x=-6$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+6)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+6)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+6)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+6)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $1$ | $14$ | $60$ | $72$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-6$ | $-48$ | $-72$ |\n|  | $1$ | $8$ | $12$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+6}={x}^{2}+8x+12,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+6)({x}^{2}+8x+12).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+6)(x+6)(x+2) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x+6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294544 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1069 "q-278326" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1071 "The multiplicity of the root $x=-10$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+300x+1000$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1072 #uuid "fa72ed1e-5e63-511a-a7a4-b3bea1a8555c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294988 1074 "Since $x=-10$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+10)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+10)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-10$ | $1$ | $30$ | $300$ | $1000$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-10$ | $-200$ | $-1000$ |\n|  | $1$ | $20$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+10}={x}^{2}+20x+100,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+10)({x}^{2}+20x+100).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+10)(x+10)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+10)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-10$ has multiplicity $3.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294312 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1069 "q-278347" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1071 "![](images/89/89914bff0203020e476acedc399cfdcc4a546bac9ee747f01951019e8d85c85d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1072 #uuid "4480a5e2-eb6c-5cde-a9fd-1943b21f43d8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294989 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(2,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294100 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294110 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294736 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294786 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1069 "q-278353" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1071 "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1072 #uuid "0916b8d4-936c-51d6-aa90-898aaf5837e5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294990 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18} & =2 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-5,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $(-5,0)$ and $(7,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294220 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294363 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294677 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294768 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1069 "q-278354" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1071 "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-(y+1)^{2}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1072 #uuid "7e70d279-ddec-5e8e-9d57-eadc04aa3a0c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294991 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-(0+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8} & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-6,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $(-6,0)$ and $(2,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294190 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294753 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1069 "q-278358" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1071 "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1072 #uuid "5c790d85-3512-5001-a574-2d227d5e2898" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294992 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8} & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(4,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294076 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294337 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294488 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294696 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1069 "q-278363" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1071 "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $6{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=30$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1072 #uuid "caf63029-110c-5e5e-ab8d-bb58a2ab4f5f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294993 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =30 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-5(0)^{2} & =30 \\\\ 6{y}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{5}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294146 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294214 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294386 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294815 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1069 "q-278364" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1071 "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1072 #uuid "80180fd4-af04-50e1-bba0-8e167a16e61a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294994 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =5\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,8)$ and $(0,2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293941 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294130 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294607 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294738 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1069 "q-278365" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1071 "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(y-1)^{2}-4(x+1)^{2}=5$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1072 #uuid "a10af876-be08-5c12-a9d2-2b12ed97a0d0" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294995 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}-4(x+1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-4(0+1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(0,-2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294276 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294622 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1069 "q-278367" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1071 "The $y$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}-(y-2)^{2}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1072 #uuid "c87d37d5-55cd-5535-9149-207dbe1c0b76" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294996 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}-(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{16}-(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ 1-(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ -(y-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-2 & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,2).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294563 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294644 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1069 "q-278368" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1071 "The $y$-intercept of the hyperbola $(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1072 #uuid "6af01ce6-2c3f-5f65-8ddc-d00d6f89b125" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294997 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (0-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+3 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,-3).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294169 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294453 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1069 "q-278371" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1071 "The hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ intersects the line $x=-3$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1072 #uuid "0135d9ff-5368-56ef-b879-e7fafdfd541f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294998 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=-3,$ we substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the horizontal line $x=-3$ at $(-3,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294165 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294209 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294553 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294746 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1069 "q-278372" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the points where the hyperbola $3{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18$ intersects the line $x=3$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1072 #uuid "1eec9b03-4f6f-5157-9af8-654600be321f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186294999 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=3,$ we substitute $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 3(3)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 27-9{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ -9{y}^{2} & =18-27 \\\\ -9{y}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=3$  at the points $(3,1)$ and $(3,-1).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294295 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294369 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294514 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294713 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1069 "q-278373" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ intersects the line $x=-5$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1072 #uuid "7ec6528c-c998-5407-b8e0-f34a37c6bb84" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295000 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=-5,$ we substitute $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1-\\frac{25}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm 1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=-5$  at the points $(-5,1.5)$ and $(-5,-1.5).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293968 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294154 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1069 "q-278374" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1$ intersects the line $y=x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1072 #uuid "61ece0da-9c0b-5643-afdc-8f35ca2ac1ce" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295001 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{12{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294362 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294771 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1069 "q-278375" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y=-x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1072 #uuid "fdeea969-9dd6-56b7-85b7-d4a36069d1e2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295002 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=-x,$ we first replace $y$ with $-x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-x)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-4$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294637 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294645 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1069 "q-278378" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $8{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}=27$ intersects the line $y=x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1072 #uuid "20c55345-03f5-539b-b65b-92b19d8c95b5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295003 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =27 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-3$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294083 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294221 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1069 "q-278379" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=20$ intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{5}x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1072 #uuid "e3924c5a-e5b9-5636-819e-461c9a4e5ffe" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295004 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{5}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\sqrt{5}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ (\\sqrt{5}x)^{2}-{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-\\sqrt{5}$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294316 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294436 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1069 "q-278380" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1072 #uuid "72ce3236-2491-5416-a8b9-d60bec69a31f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295005 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\frac{x}{4},$ we first replace $y$ with $\\frac{x}{4}$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-4$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293980 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294198 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1069 "q-278383" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1071 "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $14{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}=64$ intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{2}x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1072 #uuid "c8f9741f-55e8-540b-9683-1ea533e2d292" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295006 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{2}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\sqrt{2}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-5(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2} & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-5(2{x}^{2}) & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-10{x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-4$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294054 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294059 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294156 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294419 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1069 "q-278384" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1071 "From **left to right**, the points where the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=16$ intersects the line $y=2.5$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1072 #uuid "7096e183-2bac-525e-a16f-e109d122f25b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295007 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=2.5,$ we substitute $y=2.5$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 4(2.5)^{2}-{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 25-{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & =16-25 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $y=2.5$  at the points $(-3,2.5)$ and $(3,2.5).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294160 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294306 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1069 "q-278386" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1071 "The hyperbola $6{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}=24$ intersects the line $x=2$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1072 #uuid "9eac51bb-9a5d-5f69-9359-6ffde34dd5ad" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295008 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 6(2)^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 24-8{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=2$  at $(2,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294559 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294822 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1069 "q-278387" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1071 "The hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ intersects the line $x=1$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1072 #uuid "c767294f-ea32-5b5b-87be-45853c02b18d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295009 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=1,$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=1$  at $(1,0).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294223 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1069 "q-278388" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1071 "![](images/8d/8d6064ed62a5bdac5bac8c77e58617a5179d71ffe6cd0dc25f902af5e56bae07.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1072 #uuid "2193f080-b485-525a-882b-6b126469f525" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295010 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294106 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294668 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1069 "q-278389" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1071 "The hyperbola ${y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15}=1$ intersects the line $y=-1$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1072 #uuid "b74e5322-cd38-5dc8-9b0d-b992f60b6417" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295011 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=-1,$ we substitute $y=-1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $y=-1$  at $(0,-1).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293960 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1069 "q-278395" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1071 "![](images/33/33adaa159f0926038c6b04a750b6a61b8a18e0fe2df9c6c3c581c139e27b2c95.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1072 #uuid "c287975d-2210-588b-808c-37d4aa02c574" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295012 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(6,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294585 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1069 "q-278398" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1071 "![](images/44/441ee336bc10f5787a5956a6c118cde9faaa0485c3c2f8a6adb9dd93903b3972.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1072 #uuid "58d53b95-48c4-5460-b91e-ffee8730045f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295013 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(0,5),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294026 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294495 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1069 "q-278401" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{7}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1072 #uuid "a639bf2b-c67d-544a-8384-16c5fc84da46" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295014 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=6$ and $k=-5,$ so the center is located at $(6,-5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=7.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=7,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{7}=2\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294272 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294382 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1069 "q-278402" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1072 #uuid "072c1bea-c8aa-5be0-9fff-eabf53408ea5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295015 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-7$ and $k=-5,$ so the center is located at $(-7,-5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=11.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=11,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{11}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{11},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{11}=2\\sqrt{11},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294009 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294475 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1069 "q-278403" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1072 #uuid "f193e9d2-f5fe-5d88-ae37-9891fd4ddd44" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295016 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(1,6).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=15$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=15,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{15}=2\\sqrt{15}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294281 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294671 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1069 "q-278404" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1072 #uuid "6a4cc48c-e200-5367-abd6-f0488138a7ac" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295017 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=3$ and $k=-8,$ so the center is located at $(3,-8).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has 