 & =28x \\\\ 4p & =28 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(7){t}^{2},\\,y=2(7)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2},\\,y=14t.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294492 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294806 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1069 "q-280233" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1071 "![](images/d8/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1072 #uuid "101558b0-e03b-5b3f-a1c3-2d14536fe534" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295127 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294396 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1069 "q-280257" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1071 "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(2,4)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1072 #uuid "7c66e491-2176-5bbe-88aa-7555cf4520f9" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295128 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294383 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1069 "q-280260" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1071 "![](images/20/200dcd87827ae9683ba0ecb3b284509b31d32f9e0dc1b9736a260d2f7d71b0a0.png)\n\nRounded to **two decimal places**, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1072 #uuid "8878e119-056d-550e-93df-4c14bd85326c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295129 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5, \\\\ a & =BC=5, \\\\ m∠B & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =12.5\\cdot (0.866\\,025) \\\\  & ≈10.83,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to **two decimal places**." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294023 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294236 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1069 "q-280268" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1071 "A skier glides down a smooth slope starting from the origin with a constant velocity of $(6i-1j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Determine the skier's position vector$(r)$ after $5$ seconds.\n\n$r=(${{field-1}}  \n$i\\,\\,-$  \n{{field-2}}  \n$j)\\text{m}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1072 #uuid "714116c6-9ad6-5b3c-a139-1604a76f186f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295130 1074 "When a particle (or object) moves with constant velocity $u,$ the position vector at time $t$ is given by the equation\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position vector.\n\nIn this case, the skier starts at the origin, so\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=6i-1j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the skier after $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(6i-1j)(5) \\\\  & =(\\,30\\,i-5\\,j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294642 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1069 "q-280279" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1071 "![](images/86/866f869f43e2663c2f63a485049f84ae059e2fdcb73db0445eb355491957c66a.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Then,  $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1072 #uuid "95d92df8-f5a2-5d44-90e8-e2bcbdb9b2d6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295131 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =5b\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 25\\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & =5b\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\ 5b & =25 \\\\ b & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=5\\,\\text{ cm}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294237 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1069 "q-280282" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1071 "![](images/5c/5c0bf3060129e2a03dab0fcd2a6f0f6068d1ec565143b83d11ad4d8ae1b41b85.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the acute triangle $△ABC$ is $3{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ Then, the measure of $∠A$ is{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1072 #uuid "13cfc4c2-60f9-5bce-a135-f2772aeb4d9b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295132 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ mm}, \\\\ b & =AC=4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 3 & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294423 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1069 "q-280314" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(3,-2,1),$ $C(-1,4,-1),$ and $D(2,1,0).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1072 #uuid "227e2bc0-7de3-5c22-93a6-04542a843855" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295133 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i-2j+k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+4j-k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =-2i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+j)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(8-1)+2(-4-1)+3(-2-4) \\\\  & =14-10-18 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-14|=14.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294824 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1069 "q-280321" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,-3,2),$ $B(4,-1,5),$ $C(-2,0,1),$ and $D(3,-2,3).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1072 #uuid "0c8b632f-0961-529e-b3d4-1d020cf7c2fd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295134 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i-j+5k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i-2j+3k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1)-2((-3)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(3+1)-2(-3+2)+3(-3-6) \\\\  & =12+2-27 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-13|=13.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294599 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1069 "q-280337" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{2},\\,y=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1072 #uuid "079f9137-5a0c-5af6-8a5a-65b4ea290906" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295135 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=9x.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293985 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1069 "q-280351" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1071 "Let $c\\times a=3i-2j$ and $c\\times b=4i+j.$ Find $c\\times (a+2b).$\n\n$c\\times (a+2b)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1072 #uuid "77730a53-5e90-5c0a-a69e-20df2d022292" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295136 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\nc\\times (a+2b)=c\\times a+c\\times (2b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\nc\\times a+c\\times (2b)=c\\times a+2(c\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\times a+2(c\\times b) & =(3i-2j)+2(4i+j) \\\\  & =3i-2j+8i+2j \\\\  & =11\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294598 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1069 "q-280361" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-2t,\\,y=-\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1072 #uuid "0747d15d-d6e8-5fd0-9396-008f2449d6d3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295137 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{3y}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-3y.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294556 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1069 "q-280365" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-10{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1072 #uuid "1c1e3b25-cdde-5be3-a5b1-3b01498e6ea9" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295138 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x}{10},\\,t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x}{10}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{x}{10} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}x$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293965 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294140 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294571 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1069 "q-280381" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1071 "Let $b\\times a=⟨6,0,-3⟩.$ Find $a\\times (3a-2b).$\n\n$a\\times (3a-2b)=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1072 #uuid "34251e9f-6d6e-5593-b05c-d3a2ed14c7e5" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295139 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\na\\times (3a-2b)=a\\times (3a)-a\\times (2b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\na\\times (3a)-a\\times (2b)=3(a\\times a)-2(a\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $a\\times a=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\times a)-2(a\\times b) & =3(0)-2(-b\\times a) \\\\  & =2(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(b\\times a) & =2⟨6,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294252 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294516 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294557 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1069 "q-280385" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1071 "Let $b\\times a=2i-j+4k.$ Find $a\\times (b-3a).$\n\n$a\\times (b-3a)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1072 #uuid "3b5ecce5-4519-5318-90df-e9ee9bf806a2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295140 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\na\\times (b-3a)=a\\times b-a\\times (3a)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\na\\times b-a\\times (3a)=a\\times b-3(a\\times a)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $a\\times a=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b-3(a\\times a) & =-b\\times a-3(0) \\\\  & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-b\\times a & =-(2i-j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i+1j+-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294506 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1069 "q-280387" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1071 "If $H=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1072 #uuid "6794ccbb-8ffd-5507-a0b5-d0921c89d7f6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295141 1074 "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-7|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-7(0-(-20))+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot (20)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-140-6 \\\\  & =-146\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294147 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1069 "q-280390" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1072 #uuid "ee0a6b76-25b3-5557-92d9-083561656751" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295142 1074 "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2(0-0)-3(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294468 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295143 1069 "q-280393" 13194139533