1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294651 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1069 "q-280400" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1071 "If $W=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1072 #uuid "4c583368-3601-5727-a2d2-942c7b9558d3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295145 1074 "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(4\\cdot 1-6\\cdot (-3))+2((-1)(1)-6\\cdot 2)+3((-1)(-3)-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =5(4+18)+2(-1-12)+3(3-8) \\\\  & =5(22)+2(-13)+3(-5) \\\\  & =110-26-15 \\\\  & =69\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294477 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1069 "q-280403" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $det(A)=37$, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1072 #uuid "b8c1908a-4170-5ad4-8920-81bd7dfc6050" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295146 1074 "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}x & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & x \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(x\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)-(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(x-20)-(3+8) \\\\  & =2x-40-11 \\\\  & =2x-51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $37,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-51 & =37 \\\\ 2x & =88 \\\\ x & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294712 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1069 "q-280404" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1071 "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is singular.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1072 #uuid "b09153b7-90f9-5996-a92d-2d17cff9a754" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295147 1074 "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =|\\begin{aligned}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(k+1)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|-2k|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(k+1)(5\\cdot 1-0\\cdot k)-2k(0\\cdot 1-0\\cdot -2)+3(0\\cdot k-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5)-2k(0)+3(10) \\\\  & =5k+5+30 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-35 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293964 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293970 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1069 "q-280406" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1071 "Given that $\\frac{6-7\\text{i}}{3-2\\text{i}}=2p-5q\\text{i}$, then$\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$q=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1072 #uuid "516e7047-aa78-5f5c-ae21-61e57c17d895" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295148 1074 "First, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-7\\text{i}}{3-2\\text{i}} & =2p-5q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{(6-7\\text{i})(3+2\\text{i})}{(3-2\\text{i})(3+2\\text{i})} & =2p-5q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{18+12\\text{i}-21\\text{i}-14{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} & =2p-5q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{18-9\\text{i}-14(-1)}{13} & =2p-5q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{32-9\\text{i}}{13} & =2p-5q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{32}{13}-\\frac{9}{13}\\text{i} & =2p-5q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{32}{13}=2p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-\\frac{9}{13}=-5q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{16}{13}$ and $q=\\frac{9}{65}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294061 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294785 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1069 "q-280441" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1071 "If  $-3+2\\text{i}=\\frac{p+q\\text{i}}{1-4\\text{i}},$ then$\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$q=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1072 #uuid "3448c62f-0e33-5f6a-bd25-4433d175a9b9" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295149 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1-4\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i}=\\frac{p+q\\text{i}}{1-4\\text{i}} \\\\ (-3+2\\text{i})(1-4\\text{i}) & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ -3+12\\text{i}+2\\text{i}-8{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ -3+8+14\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ 5+14\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n5=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=14.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294745 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294798 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1069 "q-280488" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1071 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $(6i+2j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ If its initial position is ${r}_{0}=(3i-5j)\\,\\text{cm},$ find its position vector $r$ after $5$ seconds.\n\n$r=(${{field-1}}  \n$i\\,\\,+$  \n{{field-2}}  \n$j)\\text{cm}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1072 #uuid "2c5272cd-ab3a-581d-966f-2edbb774f479" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295150 1074 "When a particle (or object) moves with constant velocity $u,$ the position vector at time $t$ is given by the equation\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position vector.\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=3i-5j,\\,u=6i+2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(3i-5j)+(6i+2j)(5) \\\\  & =3i-5j+30i+10j \\\\  & =(\\,33\\,i+5\\,j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293989 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294010 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295151 1069 "q-280491" 13194139533