2 #uuid "85339780-4462-5a6b-b231-8c0f5b504e2b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295161 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=-6i+8j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the falcon after $t=1\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(-6i+8j)(1) \\\\  & =(-6i+8j)\\,\\text{mi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the falcon to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|-6i+8j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{mi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294619 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1069 "q-280531" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1071 "A car accelerates from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $(3i-4j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Find the distance from the car to the origin$(|r|)$ after $4$ seconds.\n\n$\\,|r|=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1072 #uuid "eb9e6983-09f6-5006-b1d1-f20aab350417" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295162 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=2i,\\,a=3i-4j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the car after $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(2i)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(3i-4j)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =8i+8(3i-4j) \\\\  & =8i+24i-32j \\\\  & =(32i-32j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the car to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|32i-32j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(32)^{2}+(-32)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1024+1024} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2048} \\\\  & =32\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294519 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1069 "q-280533" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1071 "An object moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(-2i+4j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(3i+j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Find the distance from the object to the origin$(|r|)$ after $2$ seconds.\n\n$\\,|r|=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1072 #uuid "f1c9acb4-741e-5645-9698-9919548e168e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295163 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=3i+j,\\,a=-2i+4j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the object after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(3i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2i+4j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(6i+2j)+\\frac{1}{2}(-8i+16j) \\\\  & =(6i+2j)+(-4i+8j) \\\\  & =(2i+10j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the object to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|2i+10j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{104} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{26}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293988 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1069 "q-280535" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1071 "A cyclist begins at the position $(2i-j)\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $(3i+j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ and an acceleration of $(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Find the distance from the cyclist to the origin $(|r|)$ after $2$ seconds.\n\n$\\,|r|=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1072 #uuid "8295acdd-3f3f-55bd-ad12-8467c3b7c98c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295164 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=2i-j,\\,u=3i+j,\\,a=i+2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the cyclist after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(2i-j)+(3i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(i+2j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(2i-j)+(6i+2j)+2(i+2j) \\\\  & =(2i-j)+(6i+2j)+(2i+4j) \\\\  & =(10i+5j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the cyclist to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|10i+5j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294108 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1069 "q-280537" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1071 "A helicopter starts from the position $(5i+2j)\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $(-i+3j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ and an acceleration of $(i-j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Find the distance from the helicopter to the origin $(|r|)$ after $2$ seconds.\n\n$\\,|r|=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1072 #uuid "2fcd7c3e-ac3b-5c90-ab32-d7f01e693c24" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295165 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=5i+2j,\\,u=-i+3j,\\,a=i-j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the helicopter after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(5i+2j)+(-i+3j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(5i+2j)+(-2i+6j)+2(i-j) \\\\  & =(5i+2j)+(-2i+6j)+(2i-2j) \\\\  & =(5i+6j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the helicopter to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|5i+6j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{61}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294345 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294695 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1069 "q-280540" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1071 "Given that the determinant of $[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is $32$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1072 #uuid "854d343a-6ceb-5dbc-aed9-5a3bba384840" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295166 1074 "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}x & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(-2\\cdot 0-(-1)(4))-3(x\\cdot 0-(-1)(1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(4)-3(1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8-3 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-11 & =32 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =43 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{43}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{43}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294339 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294457 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1069 "q-280541" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1071 "Given that the determinant of $[\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is $40$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1072 #uuid "f11851f4-08f0-546e-8ec7-83f1d8f0d049" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295167 1074 "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x|\\begin{aligned}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2x|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}3 & x \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 7)+5(3-7x) \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(-22)+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+44x+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+5x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $40$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x+15 & =40 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-25 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4(2)(-25)}}{2(2)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5+\\sqrt{225}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-5$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293974 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294494 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1069 "q-280572" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1071 "If $-7+6\\text{i}=a+b\\text{i}$, then$\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1072 #uuid "04e0caaf-40c1-52ab-bc09-7102b0a7e2e6" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295168 1074 "First, we equate the real parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-7$ and $b=6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294309 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294504 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1069 "q-280585" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1071 "If  $\\sqrt{5}+6\\text{i}=a+b\\text{i}$,  then$\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1072 #uuid "222d4951-a367-5fd3-aa55-b8672cb518d3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295169 1074 "First, we equate the real parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294462 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1069 "q-280670" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $y=⟨0,1,-1⟩$, and $z=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1072 #uuid "fbb0f86b-7cc9-5ea8-b795-4408792fb13b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295170 1074 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1-4-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294639 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1069 "q-280671" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨1,1,2⟩$, $y=⟨-3,2,0⟩$, and $z=⟨4,-2,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1072 #uuid "8009ac60-6b35-55bb-b7cb-31b4072308ca" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295171 1074 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))-1((-3)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 4)+2((-3)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2+3-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|1|=1.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294122 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1069 "q-280712" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1071 "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $b=⟨0,5,0⟩$, and $c=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1072 #uuid "783ca205-66c5-547b-848c-076696d6e4cf" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295172 1074 "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0)+3(-20) \\\\  & =30-0-60 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-30|=30.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293981 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1069 "q-280716" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1071 "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $x=⟨3,1,4⟩$, $y=⟨0,2,5⟩$, and $z=⟨6,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1072 #uuid "c8ebdcbf-5834-5ffb-a71e-6b022b6fe2f1" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295173 1074 "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0)-(0\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)+4(0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =6+30-48 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293948 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1069 "q-280720" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨2,0,k⟩$, $y=⟨1,4,3⟩$, and $z=⟨0,2,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1072 #uuid "02a24008-f4b2-51f6-bfa2-34173306dcaa" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295174 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|-0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|+k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2)+0+k(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(20-6)+2k \\\\  & =28+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ 28+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-28 \\\\ k & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-14.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294021 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1069 "q-280722" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,3,0⟩$, $b=⟨0,4,2⟩$, and $c=⟨2,k,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1072 #uuid "61f3e6a0-31a0-537c-a9de-e6c7ae59826e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295175 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\na\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2)=(20-2k)+12 \\\\  & =32-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 32-2k & =0 \\\\ -2k & =-32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=16.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294425 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1069 "q-280729" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,k,k⟩$, $b=⟨0,3,2⟩$, and $c=⟨4,0,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1072 #uuid "ee4f8503-0b7f-5200-9b9f-9ac97e166c2a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295176 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & k & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|+k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)-k(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)+k(0\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =15+8k-12k \\\\  & =15-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 15-4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{4}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294199 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294646 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1069 "q-280735" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨4,{k}^{2},2⟩$, $b=⟨1,5,3⟩$, and $c=⟨2,1,0⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1072 #uuid "85dbcbc6-7e0b-5364-ba3b-2e4b14e4021a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295177 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & {k}^{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-{k}^{2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 1)-{k}^{2}(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2)+2(1\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4(-3)-{k}^{2}(0-6)+2(1-10) \\\\  & =-12+6{k}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =6{k}^{2}-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet the triple product equal to 0:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{k}^{2}-30 & =0 \\\\ 6{k}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =5 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{5}$ or $k=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294166 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294293 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294710 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1069 "q-280834" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1071 "Let $a\\times b=⟨2,-5,-1⟩.$ Find $6b\\times (2a-b).$\n\n$6b\\times (2a-b)=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1072 #uuid "e746ff9c-9f50-594b-8773-1ffdd380a3c1" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295178 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n6b\\times (2a-b)=(6b)\\times (2a)-(6b)\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6b)\\times (2a)-(6b)\\times b & =(6\\cdot 2)(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b) \\\\  & =12(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b) & =12(-a\\times b)-6(0) \\\\  & =-12(a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $a\\times b$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12(a\\times b) & =-12⟨2,-5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-24,60,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294248 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1069 "q-281023" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,2),$ $B(3,4,5),$ $C(6,2,4),$ and $D(5,3,6).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1072 #uuid "35b9e288-8b36-5336-a7cf-8f490e426c0c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295179 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+5k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =2i+4j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,4,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(6i+2j+4k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =5i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(5i+3j+6k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =4i+3j+4k \\\\  & =⟨4,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3)-4(5\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 4)+3(5\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2(8-6)-4(20-8)+3(15-8) \\\\  & =4-48+21 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-23|=23.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294603 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1069 "q-281025" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,0,2),$ $B(5,1,3),$ $C(4,3,5),$ and $D(3,0,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1072 #uuid "143e14fb-6cbe-5d90-85cc-144627915d33" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295180 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5i+1j+3k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+5k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i+5k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =3k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)-1(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)+1(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(9)-1(3)+1(0) \\\\  & =18-3+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294550 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1069 "q-281029" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,1,3),$ $B(0,5,5),$ $C(3,4,5),$ and $D(2,0,2).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1072 #uuid "7ad6a5b8-f3d8-5275-887d-fa59251ace92" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295181 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5j+5k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+5k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+2k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =2i-1j-1k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(3\\cdot -1-2\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294693 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1069 "q-281030" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(-1,0,1),$ $B(2,-3,4),$ $C(-2,-2,2),$ and $D(0,-1,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1072 #uuid "c74fea15-7593-5e2d-a899-51b33896c8fd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295182 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+4k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =3i-3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-2j+2k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+1k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-j+5k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =i-j+4k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-8+1)+3(-4-1)+3(1-(-2)) \\\\  & =-21-15+9 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-27|=27.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294414 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294681 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1069 "q-281085" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S(2,-1)$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${S}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1072 #uuid "423a710d-2505-558c-badc-6a2f314dce98" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295183 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(14,-7).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294394 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294715 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1069 "q-281092" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${Y}^{′}$, the image of $Y(-2,-3)$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${Y}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1072 #uuid "c22179b9-c0f6-5d75-8b5d-9beb46bf3a76" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295184 1074 "To find the image of $Y$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $Y:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${Y}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${Y}^{′}(-2,11).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294700 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1069 "q-281113" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1071 "Consider the line $y=x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-7.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1072 #uuid "c3354465-52d7-54d8-a72d-f1b909cf8091" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295185 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{4-3}{5-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-9 & =x-2 \\\\ x-3y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294444 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1069 "q-281121" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1071 "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-3.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1072 #uuid "f4f36a8b-b618-5a3e-9b6e-89535fcd8027" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295186 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(7,-2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{-2+1}{7-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-2) \\\\ 5y+5 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+5y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294435 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1069 "q-281130" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1071 "Consider the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-81.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1072 #uuid "35e1a6de-f59e-590b-982f-e929e143b020" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295187 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-3,-6)$ and ${B}^{′}(7,-13).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{-13+6}{7-(-3)}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =-\\frac{7}{10}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10y+60 & =-7x-21 \\\\ 7x+10y & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294334 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1069 "q-281140" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1071 "Consider the line $y=2x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=12.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1072 #uuid "b0a3617f-2016-58e8-8cbb-ffcc1ac3b148" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295188 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=2x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(0,-6)$ and ${B}^{′}(2,-1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{-1+6}{2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{5}{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =\\frac{5}{2}x \\\\ 2y+12 & =5x \\\\ 5x-2y & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294474 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294546 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1069 "q-281168" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1071 "If  $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}-\\text{i}}=p+\\text{i}q,$ then$\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$q=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1072 #uuid "2c0ee0a5-b717-544e-a3f6-bd87393f32c3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295189 1074 "First, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}-\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{(\\sqrt{2}-\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{2+1} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{3} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $\\frac{5}{3}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294505 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1069 "q-281208" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1071 "If $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 11)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1072 #uuid "3fb037fe-0d3b-5cc8-9433-7ceae39816ec" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295190 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 11)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>11)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=12,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>11),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =P(X\\in {12}) \\\\  & =f(12) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-12} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & ≈1-0.008 \\\\  & =0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294708 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1069 "q-281236" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1071 "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=15.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1072 #uuid "a1100a3d-fd94-5a66-954c-254516d90d05" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295191 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294211 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1069 "q-281241" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1071 "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-5.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1072 #uuid "9dd45d0e-8e41-509d-8f54-e8b83cfbdfbd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295192 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-1,1)$ and ${B}^{′}(0,5).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{5-1}{0+1}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 4x-y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294808 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1069 "q-281256" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1071 "The sum of all nine $9$th roots of unity is equal to$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1072 #uuid "3461836b-8f99-5ad9-9313-bf1f369ff397" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295193 1074 "Recall that the principal $9$th root of unity is \n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/9}\\ne 1,\n$$\n\nand that any $9$th root of unity is of the form ${z}^{k}$ for $k=0,1,...,8.$  Also, we have ${z}^{9}=1.$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of a geometric sequence, we obtain that the sum of all eight roots is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+z+{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}+{z}^{4}+{z}^{5}+{z}^{6}+{z}^{7}+{z}^{8} & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1-{z}^{9})}{1-z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1-1)}{1-z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0}{1-z} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294136 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1069 "q-281297" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1072 #uuid "679c7a26-b868-5bf3-99b1-a5271d659ffa" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295194 1074 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=2p+3q\\text{i}$ is also a root." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293959 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1069 "q-281300" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=4-2r\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1072 #uuid "5c9458f8-c931-589f-8969-4e0e0e97d0f2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295195 1074 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=4-2r\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=4+2r\\text{i}$ is also a root." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294093 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1069 "q-281311" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=8\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1072 #uuid "c146b832-bd24-575f-8109-577e88c31e1b" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295196 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=8\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-8\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8\\text{i})(x+8\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(8\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-64(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294216 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1069 "q-281325" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1072 #uuid "49efb83b-7a08-50be-95c4-e45b3ab132e1" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295197 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{7\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294126 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1069 "q-281332" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1072 #uuid "10040f3a-1927-5f73-8f48-332cfb78c0f8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295198 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294210 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1069 "q-281333" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $16,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1072 #uuid "d6f8e661-f438-5c1a-9465-785304aa2cfb" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295199 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{16}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $16.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $16.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8}) & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294289 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1069 "q-281343" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=2+5\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1072 #uuid "97e1eff6-f901-5832-9878-783ff27fd149" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295200 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+5\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=2-5\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+5\\text{i}))(x-(2-5\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+5\\text{i})-x(2-5\\text{i})+(2+5\\text{i})(2-5\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-\\cancel{5x\\text{i}}-2x+\\cancel{5x\\text{i}}+4-25{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294443 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1069 "q-281348" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1072 #uuid "1b8164db-91f3-5b46-a0cb-865e547c1d79" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295201 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})-x(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})+(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+4-7{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294593 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1069 "q-281349" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1072 #uuid "56a0b70c-30bb-5c38-bfa2-b308f36fef54" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295202 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x-\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}+2-9{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+2+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294667 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1069 "q-281362" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1072 #uuid "a201b4c7-35a3-5e1f-b334-ac5af2130fa7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295203 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}-2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}+4-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-20x+35 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294594 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294685 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294686 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1069 "q-281473" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1071 "Solve the inequality $(3x-4)(1-x)(x+2)>0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1072 #uuid "731bca49-a35c-5227-8020-a1c421fe7d7c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295204 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(3x-4)(1-x)(x+2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,1),\\,(1,\\frac{4}{3}),\\,(\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$ | $(\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(1,\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(1,\\frac{4}{3}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294314 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294562 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294780 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294794 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1069 "q-281528" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1071 "Let $T$ be the linear transformation with matrix representation $T$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{RS}$ has endpoints at $R(0,2)$ and $S(-1,1).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints $\\overset{―}{{R}^{′}{S}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{RS}$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${R}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,$ and $\\,{S}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}}\n$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1072 #uuid "5ebc7843-5c4b-5817-bcef-0dd42f19ba23" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295205 1074 "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{RS}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{RS}:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are 