ormation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=16.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295212 1072 #uuid "f2ef5880-c743-5e3b-81b9-cc6ecf11b073" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295212 1074 "First, notice that the line $x=4$ passes through the points $G(4,0)$ and $H(4,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{′}(12,4)$ and ${H}^{′}(10,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${G}^{′}$ and ${H}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{6-4}{10-12}(x-12) \\\\ y-4 & =-1(x-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-x+12 \\\\ x+y & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294228 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1069 "q-281580" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1071 "Consider the line $y=-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-8.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1072 #uuid "4c349395-df7b-587e-b1ad-c70a17b7ecf2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295213 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=-1$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(0,2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,3).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3-2}{4-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-8 & =x \\\\ x-4y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294829 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1069 "q-281581" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1071 "Consider the line $y=3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=21.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1072 #uuid "3d868d9a-891d-5483-acd7-41d25e644ee4" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295214 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=3$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,3)$ and ${Q}^{′}(10,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1-3}{10-9}(x-9) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-2x+18 \\\\ 2x+y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294004 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1069 "q-281582" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1071 "Consider the line $y=2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=14.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1072 #uuid "bd4cc3a5-f3b8-5388-adc4-aea0319b6a56" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295215 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=2$ passes through the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(2,4)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1-4}{4-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-3x+6 \\\\ 3x+2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294161 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1069 "q-281583" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1071 "Consider the line $3x+2y-6=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=42.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1072 #uuid "9fca37fa-c882-5ba4-928c-7be773c41602" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295216 1074 "First, notice that the line $3x+2y-6=0$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(2,0)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,-6)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{2-(-6)}{4-9}(x-9) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{8}{-5}(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =-\\frac{8}{5}(x-9) \\\\ 5y+30 & =-8x+72 \\\\ 8x+5y & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x+5y=42\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294257 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1069 "q-281586" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1071 "Consider the line $-4x+y+1=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1072 #uuid "4baa3913-7ffc-55e5-9cc4-571282f5d439" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295217 1074 "First, notice that the line $-4x+y+1=0$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(-1,-2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(6,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{6-(-1)}(x+1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{8}{7}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y+14 & =8x+8 \\\\ 8x-7y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x-7y=6\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294387 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1069 "q-281595" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1071 "Let $P(1,-2,4)$ and $Q(-3,0,5).$ Find $|q-p|,$ where $p$ and $q$ are position vectors of $P$ and $Q$, respectively.\n\n$|q-p|=${{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1072 #uuid "7ccbe109-4080-568c-af91-e78ddda356cf" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295218 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =p=i-2j+4k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =q=-3i+5k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $q-p,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-p & =(-3i+5k)-(i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =-4i+2j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|q-p| & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294354 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294402 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1069 "q-281600" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1071 "Given that $n=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $n.$\n\n|  |  | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1072 #uuid "95640058-663f-5f13-b160-942fbde81242" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295219 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,n\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $n$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}n \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294251 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1069 "q-281689" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1071 "Let $C(2,4,-3)$ and $D(-1,0,5).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{DC}|.$\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{DC}|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1072 #uuid "8c9c72f4-ef68-58a3-b677-16ee9e68de60" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295220 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =c=2i+4j-3k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OD} & =d=-i+5k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{DC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OD} \\\\  & =c-d \\\\  & =(2i+4j-3k)-(-i+5k) \\\\  & =3i+4j-8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{DC}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293996 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294011 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294114 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294364 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294678 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1069 "q-281704" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+2)(2x-1)(1-x)(3x-4)(x-5)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$]⋃\\,[$\n{{field-5}}$,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1072 #uuid "7a5cabf8-86b8-56fd-9f92-4c0bdc290295" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295221 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(1-x)(3x-4)(x-5).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $6$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},1),\\,(1,\\frac{4}{3}),\\,(\\frac{4}{3},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ | $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$ | $(\\frac{4}{3},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)\\le 0$ on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$, $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$, and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]⋃[1,\\frac{4}{3}]⋃[5,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294427 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294602 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294779 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1069 "q-281717" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1071 "Solve the inequality $(3x-1)^{2}(2x+5)^{2}(4-x)(x-2)\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n${-\\frac{5}{2},$\n{{field-1}}\n$}⋃$\n$[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1072 #uuid "2123a721-9e73-5302-b24c-87029b4c786f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295222 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(3x-1)^{2}(2x+5)^{2}(4-x)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }2,$ and $4.$ These roots divide the number line into five subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},2),\\,(2,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(3x-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(2x+5)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(4-x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(x-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is non-negative only on the closed interval $(2,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in {-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}}⋃[2,4].$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294038 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294307 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294379 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1069 "q-281723" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-9\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$]⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1072 #uuid "aeffe2a2-0e75-5de5-a137-8a208bb1cbd1" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295223 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =(x-3)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]⋃[3,[\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294574 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294582 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1069 "q-281744" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2{x}^{3}+16\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1072 #uuid "f43e41ef-a6bd-5c80-8aed-9ba884d73302" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295224 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{3}+16 & =-2({x}^{3}-8) \\\\  & =-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$ The solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=2.$  This root divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\,,\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+2x+4$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,2).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the root is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294058 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294164 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294575 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1069 "q-281748" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1071 "Solve the inequality $10x-5{x}^{3}\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$[$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1072 #uuid "08e35889-f961-52b6-bef5-53b4e4f03325" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295225 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-5{x}^{3} & =5x(2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5x(\\sqrt{2}-x)(\\sqrt{2}+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=5x(\\sqrt{2}-x)(\\sqrt{2}+x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}),\\,(-\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})$ | $(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\sqrt{2})$ | $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $\\sqrt{2}-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $\\sqrt{2}+x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-\\sqrt{2},0]⋃[\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294320 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1069 "q-28185" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $f(x)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${x}_{1}=6\\text{i}$ and ${x}_{2}=-6\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $1.$ Find $f(x).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1072 #uuid "5595fbd6-585b-5625-b1c7-99ec2edfefea" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295226 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=6\\text{i}$ and ${x}_{2}=-6\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =(1)(x-6\\text{i})(x-(-6\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-6\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i}) & ={x}^{2}-(6\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-36{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{2}+36.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293939 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294178 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294718 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1069 "q-281969" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1071 "Let $u=⟨3,4,-1⟩$ and $v=⟨2,-5,2⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1072 #uuid "31aedb57-6eee-5721-b9e8-c3980bbe0170" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295227 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))i-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =(8-5)i-(6+2)j+(-15-8)k \\\\  & =3i-8j-23k \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294111 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294343 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294421 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1069 "q-281976" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1071 "Let $u=⟨\\sqrt{3},2,0⟩$ and $v=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1072 #uuid "21cf46ef-dcff-5226-aa0a-bbfa6e2218ee" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295228 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)i-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)j+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i+\\sqrt{3}j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294384 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294729 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294751 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1069 "q-281977" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1071 "Let $u=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0,1⟩$ and $v=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1072 #uuid "ebbf8822-5fa0-5a01-b2fc-91f9cedbb9cd" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295229 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))i-((\\frac{2}{3})(-3)-(1)(1))j+((\\frac{2}{3})(2)-(0)(1))k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(-2-1)j+(\\frac{4}{3})k \\\\  & =-2i+3j+(\\frac{4}{3})k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294240 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1069 "q-282069" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,{x}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $1.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1072 #uuid "56466fe8-ab31-5a8e-accd-bdf678af88a7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295230 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =(1)(x-(-5))(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)(x-4) & ={x}^{2}-4x+5x-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{2}+x-20.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294463 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1069 "q-282084" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=-9.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1072 #uuid "80c71663-bea3-58d5-a636-e574313693d7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295231 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=-9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})^{2} & =(-9){(x-3)}^{2} \\\\  & =-9(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(x-3)^{2} & =-9({x}^{2}-6x+9) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}+54x-81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-9{x}^{2}+54x-81.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294207 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1069 "q-282122" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=16.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1072 #uuid "4e89928f-61a7-52ec-80cc-aad994ca7831" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295232 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=16,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})^{2} & =16{(x-(-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =16(x+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16(x+4)^{2} & =16({x}^{2}+8x+16) \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+128x+256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=16{x}^{2}+128x+256.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294065 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294077 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294580 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1069 "q-282143" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1071 "Let $a=3i-4j+k$ and $b=-2i+5j+6k.$ Find $a\\times b.$\n\n$a\\times b=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1072 #uuid "5dd1f450-2fd9-570b-82aa-62d00ac130e3" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295233 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-4)(6)-(1)(5))i-((3)(6)-(1)(-2))j+((3)(5)-(-4)(-2))k \\\\  & =(-24-5)i-(18+2)j+(15-8)k \\\\  & =-29\\,i-20\\,j+7\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294350 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294564 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294664 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1069 "q-282144" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1071 "Let $a=i+2j-5k$ and $b=-3i+4j+k.$ Find $a\\times b.$\n\n$a\\times b=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1072 #uuid "334ad845-67a7-568d-adb2-3211cbc38bf2" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295234 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-(-5)(4))i-(1\\cdot 1-(-5)(-3))j+(1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =(2+20)i-(1-15)j+(4+6)k \\\\  & =22\\,i+14\\,j+10\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294416 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294501 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294803 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1069 "q-282145" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1071 "Let $a=-6i+3j+2k$ and $b=4i-j+5k.$ Find $a\\times b.$\n\n$a\\times b=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1072 #uuid "3af77c79-40bd-5add-839b-47eafec9a2aa" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295235 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot -1)i-(-6\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)j+(-6\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(15+2)i-(-30-8)j+(6-12)k \\\\  & =17\\,i+38\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294176 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294194 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294319 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1069 "q-282146" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1071 "Let $a=2i-7j+4k$ and $b=5i+3j-6k.$ Find $a\\times b.$\n\n$a\\times b=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1072 #uuid "7ca86529-b09e-5f76-bd19-11fe65ed6396" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295236 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-7)(-6)-(4)(3))i-((2)(-6)-(4)(5))j+((2)(3)-(-7)(5))k \\\\  & =(42-12)i-(-12-20)j+(6+35)k \\\\  & =30\\,i+32\\,j+41\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294292 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294385 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294432 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1069 "q-282147" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1071 "Let $a=-4i+3k$ and $b=i+2j-2k.$ Find $a\\times b.$\n\n$a\\times b=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1072 #uuid "3fcf3e73-8775-529c-878e-bbc1947caeb4" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295237 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0)(-2)-(3)(2)i-(-4)(-2)-(3)(1)j+(-4)(2)-(0)(1)k \\\\  & =(0-6)i-(8-3)j+(-8-0)k \\\\  & =-6\\,i-5\\,j-8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294542 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1069 "q-282168" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $f(y)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${y}_{1}=2+3\\text{i}$ and ${y}_{2}=2-3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $5.$ Find $f(y).$\n\n$f(y)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1072 #uuid "d32a8715-9c97-5f82-8940-4cd33a9e901a" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295238 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${y}_{1}=2+3\\text{i}$ and ${y}_{2}=2-3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=5,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(y-{y}_{1})(y-{y}_{2}) & =(5)(y-(2+3\\text{i}))(y-(2-3\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-(2+3\\text{i}))(y-(2-3\\text{i})) & =5({y}^{2}-(2+3\\text{i})y-(2-3\\text{i})y+(2+3\\text{i})(2-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =5({y}^{2}-(2+3\\text{i}+2-3\\text{i})y+({2}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2})) \\\\  & =5({y}^{2}-4y+(4+9)) \\\\  & =5({y}^{2}-4y+13) \\\\  & =5{y}^{2}-20y+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(y)=5{y}^{2}-20y+65.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294006 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1069 "q-28219" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1072 #uuid "1863d55d-bd95-51d5-93ce-914cc34a24ab" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295239 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2+\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2-\\text{i}))(x-(2+\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\text{i})-x(2-\\text{i})+(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-x\\text{i}-2x+x\\text{i}+4-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294003 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1069 "q-28224" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3-2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5$, find the equation." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1072 #uuid "bbd0c2fe-1503-5ee3-9aba-ef09db38d12f" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295240 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3+2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3-2\\text{i}))(x-(-3+2\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3+2\\text{i})-x(-3-2\\text{i})+(-3-2\\text{i})(-3+2\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-2x\\text{i}+3x+2x\\text{i}+9-4{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+6x+13) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+30x+65 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294278 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294682 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1069 "q-282260" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1071 "Let $a=⟨2x,3,1⟩,$ $b=⟨-2x,0,4⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{280}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1072 #uuid "a3a69df9-3422-55c4-b6e2-d44c3f81e8dc" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295241 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ -2x & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2x & 3 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(12-0)i-(8x+2x)j+(0+6x)k \\\\  & =⟨12,-10x,6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+(-10x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+100{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ \\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144} & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ 136{x}^{2}+144 & =280 \\\\ 136{x}^{2} & =136 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-1,$ $x=1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293956 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294073 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1069 "q-282261" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1071 "Let $a=⟨x,0,1⟩,$ $b=⟨-x,2,0⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{29}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1072 #uuid "8e10f888-66d4-5375-8f79-b8da98cf5664" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295242 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ x & 0 & 1 \\\\ -x & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(0+x)j+(2x+0)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-x,2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4} & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4 & =29 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294188 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294766 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1069 "q-282262" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1071 "Let $a=⟨x,3,0⟩,$ $b=⟨-x,0,1⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{49}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1072 #uuid "999eb6f6-f5c6-519d-9ac7-4875fbf986b8" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295243 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ x & 3 & 0 \\\\ -x & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}x & 3 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(x+0)j+(0+3x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,-x,3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+9 & =49 \\\\ 10{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=2.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294412 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1069 "q-282269" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1071 "Let $a=⟨x,1,1⟩,$ $b=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{10}.$ Find $x.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1072 #uuid "59e9fba3-4db6-5c48-86ed-2af0a557b731" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295244 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(3x-1)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨3,1-3x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(1-3x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11} & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-6x+11 & =10 \\\\ (3x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294624 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1069 "q-282270" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1071 "Let $a=⟨x,1,2⟩,$ $b=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{5}.$ Find $x.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1072 #uuid "f7de1193-053f-5234-9520-07973769fe4c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295245 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2x-2)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨2,2-2x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-2x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(4{x}^{2}-8x+4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (2x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293946 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294222 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1069 "q-282276" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1071 "Let $a=⟨x,4,5⟩,$ $b=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{33}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1072 #uuid "fe9d7249-168a-5810-85b9-89bad6dafc21" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295246 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ x & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}x & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(4-0)i-(x-5)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =⟨4,5-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(5-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+(25-10x+{x}^{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57} & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+57 & =33 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+24 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=4,$ $x=6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294618 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294701 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1069 "q-282278" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1071 "Let $a=⟨2x,1,4⟩,$ $b=⟨3,0,2⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{157}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1072 #uuid "d9b76997-cb19-548e-a5c8-ac57874f667e" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295247 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(4x-12)j+(0-3)k \\\\  & =⟨2,12-4x,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(12-4x)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(144-96x+16{x}^{2})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157} & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x+157 & =157 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x & =0 \\\\ 16x(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=6.$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293978 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294426 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1069 "q-282324" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1071 "Let $R(5,-3,2)$ and $S(1,-1,4).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{SR}.$\n\n$u=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1072 #uuid "4cc971df-ce67-5e9e-ab75-15176f12f14d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295248 1074 "First, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =r=5i-3j+2k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OS} & =s=i-j+4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =r-s \\\\  & =(5i-3j+2k)-(i-j+4k) \\\\  & =4i-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{SR}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{SR}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{SR}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{SR}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{SR}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\overset{\\to }{SR} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot ⟨4,-2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294256 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294434 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1069 "q-282351" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1071 "Let $A(3,-2,-1)$ and $B(3,-2,0).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $-2a+b.$\n\n$u=⟨${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}⟩$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1072 #uuid "3778c517-a7f2-54f3-a950-c1178f294186" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295249 1074 "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=3i-2j-k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $-2a+b$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a+b & =-2(3i-2j-k)+(3i-2j) \\\\  & =-6i+4j+2k+3i-2j \\\\  & =-3i+2j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{-2a+b}{|\\,-2a+b\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we divide the vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\cdot ⟨-3,2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}},\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}},\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294118 131941395