(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $1+2x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(-\\frac{1}{2},0)⋃(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294441 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294552 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1069 "q-282519" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}+16x+64\\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\infty )$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1072 #uuid "21750ac5-9e55-5964-8a89-66357b7c5240" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295251 1074 "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+16x+64 & \\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}+16x+64-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+16x+64 & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})+(16x+64) & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+4)+16(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+16)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (16-{x}^{2})(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)^{2} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(4-x)(4+x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into 3 subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(4+x)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative only on the interval $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in {-4}⋃[4,\\infty ).$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294097 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294375 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294438 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294830 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1069 "q-282526" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}\\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}.$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$]⋃\\,{$\n{{field-2}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1072 #uuid "da3b919a-86a4-56c1-9f55-bf9e1f8a3b59" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295252 1074 "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}-{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{6}-{x}^{5}-6{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({x}^{2}-x-6) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're 