T}$ is a \n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}\n matrix." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295271 1072 #uuid "86d61d63-e55b-5b80-81ba-d58323a1b38d" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295271 1074 "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m\\times n$ to $n\\times m.$  \n\nSo, since $F$ is a $6\\times 1$ matrix, the transpose ${F}^{T}$ is a $1\\times 6$ matrix." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294560 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1069 "q-28308" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1071 "![](images/43/43e5ba6bff8a20c20f8be2b18d57ad07fc030477d589a2ba43da0f2cb3816587.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of $△ABC$ shown above?" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1072 #uuid "9a5b6ec9-da91-5c7a-b81b-834324512d76" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295272 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to the given angle $∠B,$ we have the length of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and need to determine the length of the adjacent side $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({40}^{∘}) & =\\frac{20}{BC} \\\\ BC\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({40}^{∘}) & =20 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{20}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({40}^{∘})} \\\\ BC & =23.835…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot (23.835…) \\\\  & =238.350… \\\\  & ≈238,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294687 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1069 "q-28319" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1071 "![](images/57/576323c48b707bb198045ab932342e53709c7a3678282835430e506c1a9dd605.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1072 #uuid "8153b63e-3d67-59e4-91e1-bd414178da80" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295273 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={45}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{5x+5}{6x} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5x+5}{6x} \\\\ 6x & =5x+5 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =6x=6(5)=30, \\\\ BC & =5x+5=5(5)+5=30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 30\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =450.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294733 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1069 "q-28328" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1071 "Given that the lines $y=5qx+9$ and $y=-3x-8$ are perpendicular, what is the value of $q?$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1072 #uuid "fe99c4a9-356e-5f86-a2a8-a7dd24fba9f7" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295274 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nBoth lines are slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. \n\n- The slope of the line $y=5qx+9$ is $5q.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=-3x-8$ is $-3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5q\\cdot (-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294500 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1069 "q-28338" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1071 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+10y=20?$" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1072 #uuid "7542d07b-ac7a-53fd-84e7-5ac011364c8c" 13194139533410 17592186045585 true] [17592186295275 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+10y=20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+10y & =20 \\\\ 10y & =-4x+20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{10}x+\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ has a slope of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533410 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-001a0d9f-37c7-5af8-8262-d33442935b95" 17592186292945 "answer-006aefd8-5818-5b51-a528-fe508c74ecbb" 17592186292946 "answer-007697a9-04e0-596f-a626-674803af6980" 17592186292947 "answer-007e964a-afcb-5f04-a0f2-e9447ebd504f" 17592186292948 "answer-01dff99d-dc86-56c5-9f73-ff0d913f2b55" 17592186292949 "answer-02cc3022-c408-5fcb-92ab-57520548b44d" 17592186292950 "answer-0312d937-c190-5109-b9f9-5091e44baaf1" 17592186292951 "answer-033756ea-bff3-5bc0-acbd-1b44c28a80f8" 17592186292952 "answer-03643acb-7bd3-5f5f-9e7f-5c4a7f511e02" 17592186292953 "answer-037bdf92-391c-5583-ae6c-87ba5fc0121d" 17592186292954 "answer-03f234e7-1de4-5948-8702-5